WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:01.676 --> 00:36.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

00:36.716 --> 00:39.126 align:middle
Good afternoon, everyone,
and thanks for joining us.

00:40.036 --> 00:43.196 align:middle
Our country continues to face a
difficult and challenging time,

00:43.656 --> 00:46.076 align:middle
as the pandemic is causing
tremendous hardship here

00:46.076 --> 00:47.796 align:middle
in the United States
and around the world.

00:48.906 --> 00:50.226 align:middle
People have lost loved ones.

00:50.526 --> 00:52.136 align:middle
Many millions have
lost their jobs.

00:52.616 --> 00:55.366 align:middle
There is great uncertainty
about the future.

00:55.986 --> 00:58.376 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:58.376 --> 01:01.516 align:middle
to using our tools to do
whatever we can-and for as long

01:01.516 --> 01:04.326 align:middle
as it takes-to provide
some relief and stability,

01:04.716 --> 01:07.466 align:middle
to ensure that the recovery
will be as strong as possible,

01:07.966 --> 01:10.456 align:middle
and to limit lasting
damage to the economy.

01:11.036 --> 01:14.756 align:middle
The most important response
to this crisis has come

01:14.756 --> 01:16.056 align:middle
from our health-care workers.

01:16.816 --> 01:18.076 align:middle
And on behalf of
the Federal Reserve,

01:18.076 --> 01:20.216 align:middle
let me express our
sincere gratitude

01:20.216 --> 01:22.876 align:middle
to those dedicated individuals
who put themselves at risk,

01:23.326 --> 01:26.066 align:middle
day after day, in service
to others and to our nation.

01:26.956 --> 01:29.246 align:middle
Let me also thank the many
other essential workers

01:29.246 --> 01:32.496 align:middle
across the country who have
helped meet our basic needs

01:32.496 --> 01:34.656 align:middle
for goods and services
in these difficult times.

01:35.186 --> 01:38.006 align:middle
The virus and the
forceful measures taken

01:38.006 --> 01:40.616 align:middle
to control its spread have
induced a sharp decline

01:40.616 --> 01:43.336 align:middle
in economic activity and
a surge in job losses.

01:44.286 --> 01:46.916 align:middle
Indicators of spending and
production plummeted in April,

01:47.506 --> 01:50.586 align:middle
and the decline in real GDP in
the current quarter is likely

01:50.586 --> 01:52.126 align:middle
to be the most severe on record.

01:54.706 --> 01:57.536 align:middle
Even after the unexpectedly
positive May employment report,

01:58.146 --> 02:01.326 align:middle
nearly 20 million jobs have
been lost on net since February,

02:01.856 --> 02:03.296 align:middle
and the unemployment
rate has risen

02:03.296 --> 02:06.216 align:middle
about 10 percentage
points, to 13.3 percent.

02:07.506 --> 02:09.846 align:middle
As was highlighted by the
Bureau of Labor Statistics,

02:09.986 --> 02:12.936 align:middle
this figure likely understates
the extent of unemployment;

02:13.506 --> 02:16.056 align:middle
accounting for the unusually
large number of workers

02:16.056 --> 02:18.336 align:middle
who reported themselves
as employed but absent

02:18.336 --> 02:20.686 align:middle
from their jobs would
raise the unemployment rate

02:20.686 --> 02:22.416 align:middle
by about 3 percentage points.

02:23.726 --> 02:26.306 align:middle
The downturn has not fallen
equally on all Americans,

02:26.306 --> 02:28.736 align:middle
and those least able to shoulder
the burden have been the

02:28.736 --> 02:29.526 align:middle
most affected.

02:30.416 --> 02:31.466 align:middle
In particular, the rise

02:31.466 --> 02:33.726 align:middle
in joblessness has
been especially severe

02:33.726 --> 02:35.986 align:middle
for lower-wage workers,
for women,

02:36.336 --> 02:38.326 align:middle
and for African Americans
and Hispanics.

02:38.856 --> 02:42.786 align:middle
In recent weeks, some indicators
suggest a stabilization

02:42.786 --> 02:45.656 align:middle
or even a modest rebound in
some segments of the economy,

02:46.066 --> 02:48.686 align:middle
such as retail merchandise
and motor vehicle sales.

02:49.276 --> 02:52.126 align:middle
Employment rose in many
sectors of the economy in May,

02:52.706 --> 02:53.736 align:middle
and the unemployment
[rate] edged

02:53.736 --> 02:56.086 align:middle
down as some workers
returned to their jobs

02:56.086 --> 02:57.266 align:middle
from temporary layoffs.

02:57.616 --> 03:00.206 align:middle
With the easing of
social-distancing restrictions

03:00.206 --> 03:03.066 align:middle
across the country, people
are increasingly moving about,

03:03.176 --> 03:06.496 align:middle
and many businesses are resuming
operations to varying degrees.

03:06.996 --> 03:08.196 align:middle
At the same time,

03:08.196 --> 03:10.366 align:middle
many households have been
receiving stimulus payments

03:10.366 --> 03:11.506 align:middle
and unemployment benefits,

03:11.866 --> 03:13.906 align:middle
which are supporting
incomes and spending.

03:15.356 --> 03:17.736 align:middle
Activity in many parts of the
economy has yet to pick up,

03:17.736 --> 03:20.956 align:middle
however, and overall output
is far below earlier levels.

03:21.826 --> 03:23.556 align:middle
Moreover, despite
the improvements seen

03:23.556 --> 03:24.646 align:middle
in the May jobs report,

03:24.746 --> 03:26.706 align:middle
unemployment remains
historically high.

03:27.296 --> 03:31.166 align:middle
Weak demand, especially
in sectors most affected

03:31.166 --> 03:33.936 align:middle
by the pandemic, is holding
down consumer prices.

03:34.536 --> 03:37.636 align:middle
As a result, inflation has
fallen well below our symmetric

03:37.636 --> 03:38.536 align:middle
2 percent objective.

03:40.256 --> 03:42.956 align:middle
Indicators of longer-term
inflation expectations have been

03:42.956 --> 03:43.776 align:middle
fairly steady.

03:44.466 --> 03:46.606 align:middle
The extent of the
downturn and the pace

03:46.606 --> 03:48.966 align:middle
of recovery remain
extraordinarily uncertain

03:49.276 --> 03:52.716 align:middle
and will depend in large
part on our success

03:52.716 --> 03:53.926 align:middle
in containing the virus.

03:54.736 --> 03:56.326 align:middle
We all want to get
back to normal,

03:56.606 --> 03:58.986 align:middle
but a full recovery
is unlikely to occur

03:58.986 --> 04:02.006 align:middle
until people are confident
that it is safe to reengage

04:02.006 --> 04:03.516 align:middle
in a broad range of activities.

04:04.036 --> 04:06.866 align:middle
The severity of the
downturn will also depend

04:06.866 --> 04:09.476 align:middle
on the policy actions taken
at all levels of government

04:09.476 --> 04:11.866 align:middle
to provide relief and
to support the recovery

04:11.936 --> 04:13.766 align:middle
when the public health
crisis passes.

04:14.296 --> 04:17.746 align:middle
The Fed's response is
guided by our mandate

04:17.746 --> 04:20.346 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

04:20.346 --> 04:22.866 align:middle
for the American people, along
with our responsibilities

04:22.866 --> 04:24.816 align:middle
to promote the stability
of the financial system.

04:25.856 --> 04:28.226 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

04:28.226 --> 04:30.306 align:middle
to support the economy
in this challenging time.

04:31.416 --> 04:34.116 align:middle
In March, we quickly lowered
our policy interest rate

04:34.116 --> 04:37.596 align:middle
to near zero, where we expect to
keep it until we are confident

04:37.596 --> 04:40.426 align:middle
that the economy has weathered
recent events and is on track

04:40.426 --> 04:42.096 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

04:42.526 --> 04:43.766 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

04:44.636 --> 04:47.176 align:middle
We have also been taking
broad and forceful actions

04:47.176 --> 04:49.236 align:middle
to support the flow of
credit in the economy.

04:50.116 --> 04:52.986 align:middle
Without access to credit,
families could be forced

04:52.986 --> 04:55.766 align:middle
to cut back on necessities
or even lose their homes.

04:57.376 --> 04:59.676 align:middle
Businesses could be forced
to downsize or close,

04:59.856 --> 05:02.296 align:middle
resulting in further
losses of jobs and incomes

05:02.296 --> 05:03.596 align:middle
and worsening the downturn.

05:04.626 --> 05:06.986 align:middle
Preserving the flow of
credit is thus essential

05:06.986 --> 05:09.156 align:middle
for mitigating the
damage to the economy

05:09.156 --> 05:10.756 align:middle
and setting the stage
for the recovery.

05:11.776 --> 05:14.316 align:middle
Since March, we have been
purchasing sizable quantities

05:14.316 --> 05:16.816 align:middle
of Treasury and agency
mortgage-backed securities

05:17.246 --> 05:19.776 align:middle
in order to support the smooth
functioning of these markets,

05:20.136 --> 05:22.396 align:middle
which are vital to the flow
of credit in the economy.

05:23.336 --> 05:25.486 align:middle
Our ongoing purchases
have helped

05:25.486 --> 05:27.256 align:middle
to restore orderly
market conditions

05:27.496 --> 05:28.986 align:middle
and have fostered
more accommodative

05:28.986 --> 05:30.036 align:middle
financial conditions.

05:30.856 --> 05:32.526 align:middle
As market functioning
has improved

05:32.526 --> 05:34.326 align:middle
since the strains
experienced in March,

05:34.796 --> 05:37.366 align:middle
we have gradually reduced
the pace of these purchases.

05:38.066 --> 05:40.216 align:middle
To sustain smooth
market functioning

05:40.216 --> 05:43.516 align:middle
and thereby foster the effective
transmission of monetary policy

05:43.836 --> 05:47.406 align:middle
to broader financial conditions,
we will increase our holdings

05:47.406 --> 05:49.866 align:middle
of Treasury and agency
mortgage-backed securities

05:49.866 --> 05:52.586 align:middle
over coming months at
least at the current pace.

05:53.136 --> 05:56.166 align:middle
We will closely monitor
developments and are prepared

05:56.166 --> 05:57.276 align:middle
to adjust our plans

05:57.276 --> 05:59.106 align:middle
as appropriate to
support our goals.

05:59.616 --> 06:03.096 align:middle
The Federal Reserve is
also undertaking programs

06:03.096 --> 06:04.956 align:middle
to provide stability
to the financial system

06:04.956 --> 06:06.906 align:middle
and to more directly
support the flow of credit

06:06.906 --> 06:10.346 align:middle
in the economy-for households,
for businesses of all sizes,

06:10.346 --> 06:11.886 align:middle
and for state and
local governments.

06:13.046 --> 06:15.846 align:middle
These programs benefit the
economy by providing financing

06:15.846 --> 06:17.476 align:middle
where it is not otherwise
available.

06:18.306 --> 06:21.576 align:middle
In addition, by serving as a
backstop to key credit markets,

06:21.986 --> 06:23.896 align:middle
the programs can
increase the willingness

06:23.896 --> 06:25.586 align:middle
of private lenders
to extend credit.

06:26.316 --> 06:29.186 align:middle
Many of these programs rely
on emergency lending powers

06:29.186 --> 06:32.176 align:middle
that are available only in
very unusual circumstances,

06:32.436 --> 06:34.776 align:middle
such as these-those we
find ourselves in today.

06:35.336 --> 06:38.036 align:middle
We are deploying
these lending powers

06:38.036 --> 06:40.676 align:middle
to an unprecedented extent,
enabled in large part

06:40.676 --> 06:42.946 align:middle
by financial backing and
support from Congress

06:43.066 --> 06:43.916 align:middle
and from the Treasury.

06:44.386 --> 06:48.396 align:middle
We will continue to use these
powers forcefully, proactively,

06:48.396 --> 06:51.536 align:middle
and aggressively until we're
confident that we are solidly

06:51.536 --> 06:52.576 align:middle
on the road to recovery.

06:53.186 --> 06:55.906 align:middle
When the time comes, after
the crisis has passed,

06:56.466 --> 06:59.486 align:middle
we will put these emergency
tools back in the toolbox.

06:59.596 --> 07:02.956 align:middle
I would stress that
these are lending powers,

07:02.956 --> 07:04.066 align:middle
not spending powers.

07:04.366 --> 07:07.086 align:middle
The Fed cannot grant money
to particular beneficiaries.

07:07.476 --> 07:09.676 align:middle
We can only create
programs or facilities

07:09.796 --> 07:12.696 align:middle
with broad-based
eligibility to make loans

07:12.696 --> 07:15.746 align:middle
to solvent entity-entities
with the expectation

07:15.746 --> 07:17.016 align:middle
that the loans will be repaid.

07:18.066 --> 07:20.496 align:middle
Many borrowers will
benefit from these programs,

07:20.566 --> 07:21.986 align:middle
as will the overall economy.

07:22.406 --> 07:24.856 align:middle
But for many others, getting
a loan that may be difficult

07:24.856 --> 07:26.296 align:middle
to repay may not be the answer.

07:27.196 --> 07:29.876 align:middle
In these cases, direct
fiscal support may be needed.

07:30.686 --> 07:33.046 align:middle
Elected officials have
the power to tax and spend

07:33.046 --> 07:35.596 align:middle
and to make decisions about
where we, as a society,

07:35.916 --> 07:37.866 align:middle
should direct our
collective resources.

07:38.756 --> 07:41.616 align:middle
The CARES Act and other
legislation provide direct help

07:41.616 --> 07:43.796 align:middle
to people and businesses
and communities.

07:44.486 --> 07:47.456 align:middle
This direct support can make
a critical difference not just

07:47.456 --> 07:49.646 align:middle
in helping families and
businesses in a time of need,

07:50.016 --> 07:52.396 align:middle
but also in limiting
long-lasting damage

07:52.396 --> 07:53.806 align:middle
to our economy.

07:55.026 --> 07:58.316 align:middle
At this meeting, my colleagues
and I continued our discussion

07:58.316 --> 08:00.616 align:middle
of approaches for
conducting monetary policy

08:01.116 --> 08:03.356 align:middle
when the federal funds
rate is at its lower bound.

08:04.666 --> 08:07.386 align:middle
The measures we discussed
included explicit forms

08:07.386 --> 08:09.526 align:middle
of forward guidance
and asset purchases.

08:09.866 --> 08:11.606 align:middle
We used these tools
in the aftermath

08:11.606 --> 08:12.936 align:middle
of the Global Financial Crisis,

08:13.226 --> 08:15.656 align:middle
and they have become a
standard part of our toolkit.

08:17.106 --> 08:19.646 align:middle
We also reviewed the historical
and foreign experience

08:19.646 --> 08:21.856 align:middle
with targeting interest
rates along the yield curve.

08:22.696 --> 08:25.606 align:middle
Whether such an approach would
usefully complement our main

08:25.606 --> 08:27.356 align:middle
tools remains an open question.

08:27.586 --> 08:30.516 align:middle
We will continue our
discussions in upcoming meetings

08:30.586 --> 08:33.996 align:middle
and will evaluate our monetary
policy stance and communications

08:34.456 --> 08:36.506 align:middle
as more information
about the trajectory

08:36.506 --> 08:38.226 align:middle
of the economy becomes
available.

08:39.586 --> 08:42.256 align:middle
We also resumed our
regular quarterly Summary

08:42.256 --> 08:44.456 align:middle
of Economic Projections,
or the SEP.

08:44.456 --> 08:49.016 align:middle
The SEP is an input into our
deliberations, not an outcome,

08:49.586 --> 08:51.626 align:middle
and it does not represent
a Committee view.

08:52.086 --> 08:55.976 align:middle
Rather, FOMC participants write
down their individual views

08:56.026 --> 08:58.106 align:middle
of the most likely
path for the economy,

08:58.516 --> 09:00.736 align:middle
conditioned on each
participant's view

09:00.736 --> 09:02.296 align:middle
of appropriate monetary policy.

09:03.106 --> 09:06.206 align:middle
We tabulate those submissions,
and we publish them as the SEP.

09:06.206 --> 09:09.826 align:middle
Given the unusually high
level of uncertainty

09:09.826 --> 09:12.086 align:middle
about the outlook,
many participants noted

09:12.086 --> 09:14.526 align:middle
that they see a number of
reasonably likely paths

09:14.526 --> 09:17.616 align:middle
for the economy, and that
it's not possible to identify

09:17.616 --> 09:21.096 align:middle
with confidence a single path
as the "most likely" one.

09:21.966 --> 09:24.216 align:middle
Nonetheless, we believe
that regular publication

09:24.216 --> 09:27.036 align:middle
of the SEP provides
a useful perspective

09:27.096 --> 09:30.986 align:middle
on the way FOMC participants
are assessing the path ahead.

09:32.186 --> 09:35.176 align:middle
What the June SEP shows
is a general expectation

09:35.176 --> 09:37.466 align:middle
of an economic recovery
beginning in the second half

09:37.466 --> 09:40.326 align:middle
of this year and lasting over
the next couple of years,

09:40.786 --> 09:42.556 align:middle
supported by interest
rates that remain

09:42.556 --> 09:44.386 align:middle
at their current
level, near zero.

09:45.156 --> 09:47.556 align:middle
Of course, my colleagues
and I will continue

09:47.556 --> 09:50.466 align:middle
to base our policy
decisions on the full range

09:50.466 --> 09:53.336 align:middle
of plausible outcomes and
not on a particular forecast.

09:53.666 --> 09:56.756 align:middle
The risk-management
approach-this risk-management

09:56.756 --> 10:00.466 align:middle
approach is the best way we can
promote our maximum-employment

10:00.466 --> 10:01.686 align:middle
and price-stability goals

10:01.766 --> 10:04.496 align:middle
in these unusually
uncertain circumstances.

10:05.836 --> 10:08.896 align:middle
Finally, I want to
acknowledge the tragic events

10:08.896 --> 10:11.256 align:middle
that have again put a
spotlight on the pain

10:11.256 --> 10:12.916 align:middle
of racial injustice
in this country.

10:13.966 --> 10:16.076 align:middle
The Federal Reserve
serves the entire nation.

10:16.306 --> 10:18.756 align:middle
We operate in, and are part
of, many of the communities

10:18.756 --> 10:21.056 align:middle
across the country where
Americans are grappling with

10:21.316 --> 10:24.056 align:middle
and expressing themselves on
issues of racial equality.

10:25.196 --> 10:27.126 align:middle
I speak for my colleagues
throughout the Federal Reserve

10:27.126 --> 10:29.916 align:middle
System when I say that there is
no place at the Federal Reserve

10:29.916 --> 10:33.236 align:middle
for racism and there should be
no place for it in our society.

10:34.706 --> 10:37.926 align:middle
Everyone deserves the
opportunity to participate fully

10:37.926 --> 10:39.646 align:middle
in our society and
in our economy.

10:40.656 --> 10:44.056 align:middle
These principles guide us in
all we do, from monetary policy

10:44.166 --> 10:46.976 align:middle
to our focus on diversity and
inclusion in our workplace

10:47.496 --> 10:49.706 align:middle
and to our work to
ensure fair access

10:49.706 --> 10:51.136 align:middle
to credit across the country.

10:51.806 --> 10:53.306 align:middle
We will take this opportunity

10:53.306 --> 10:55.956 align:middle
to renew our steadfast
commitment to these principles.

10:56.476 --> 11:00.546 align:middle
We understand that the work of
the Fed touches communities,

11:00.546 --> 11:02.366 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

11:02.946 --> 11:05.366 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

11:06.106 --> 11:08.356 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

11:08.356 --> 11:11.116 align:middle
to support the economy and to
help assure that the recovery

11:11.386 --> 11:14.376 align:middle
from this difficult period
will be as robust as possible.

11:15.106 --> 11:15.496 align:middle
Thank you.

11:16.186 --> 11:20.716 align:middle
I look forward to our questions.

11:20.716 --> 11:23.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:23.226 --> 11:29.366 align:middle
For the first question,
Nick Timiraos.

11:29.366 --> 11:30.436 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

11:30.436 --> 11:32.266 align:middle
Yes, hi. Thanks, Chair Powell.

11:32.546 --> 11:34.656 align:middle
Nick Timiraos at the
Wall Street Journal.

11:34.936 --> 11:37.436 align:middle
I want to ask about the
economic projections,

11:37.436 --> 11:41.256 align:middle
and I realize these are more of
an educated guess at this point,

11:41.446 --> 11:44.476 align:middle
but they suggest the Committee
sees quite a large output gap

11:44.476 --> 11:45.896 align:middle
over the next two years.

11:46.366 --> 11:48.876 align:middle
And yet the Committee
did not take any steps

11:48.876 --> 11:52.146 align:middle
to date to-to reinforce
your forward guidance.

11:52.146 --> 11:54.176 align:middle
And so my questions are, first,

11:54.586 --> 11:57.046 align:middle
what are you hoping
to learn by waiting?

11:57.046 --> 12:00.106 align:middle
Second, how might that
change your response?

12:00.106 --> 12:04.006 align:middle
And, third, how close is the
Committee to reaching a decision

12:04.286 --> 12:07.346 align:middle
on a-a more concrete
forward guidance

12:07.346 --> 12:11.346 align:middle
and whether yield caps might
reinforce that guidance?

12:11.346 --> 12:12.846 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:15.416 --> 12:17.356 align:middle
So, first, I would
say that we think

12:17.356 --> 12:19.956 align:middle
that monetary policy today
is currently well positioned

12:19.956 --> 12:22.216 align:middle
to support the economy
in this challenging time.

12:22.646 --> 12:24.336 align:middle
If we didn't think
that, of course,

12:24.336 --> 12:25.676 align:middle
we would change our policy now.

12:26.236 --> 12:29.336 align:middle
As you know, we-we lowered
our policy rate very quickly,

12:29.376 --> 12:31.846 align:middle
[more] quickly than others,
to the effective lower bound.

12:32.256 --> 12:33.656 align:middle
And we've said that
we'll keep it there

12:34.006 --> 12:37.016 align:middle
until the economy has weathered
the effects of the virus and is

12:37.016 --> 12:38.416 align:middle
on track to achieve our goals.

12:39.346 --> 12:41.896 align:middle
You can see that
in-in the dot plot,

12:42.036 --> 12:46.216 align:middle
as I think you pointed
out, that overwhelmingly,

12:46.276 --> 12:49.766 align:middle
FOMC participants expect, as
their baseline expectation,

12:50.186 --> 12:52.756 align:middle
no rate increase at
least through 2022.

12:52.756 --> 12:55.176 align:middle
And if you look at
surveys of more forecasters

12:55.176 --> 12:57.626 align:middle
and market participants,
financial market prices,

12:57.626 --> 13:00.716 align:middle
et cetera, those also
appropriately reflect a long

13:00.716 --> 13:02.506 align:middle
spell with rates at the
effective lower bound.

13:02.966 --> 13:05.476 align:middle
So the first thing is, monetary
policy is in a good place,

13:05.476 --> 13:09.386 align:middle
and it's-that's well
understood in the markets.

13:10.376 --> 13:13.266 align:middle
Secondly, we've also
taken strong measures

13:13.266 --> 13:15.596 align:middle
to support the flow of credit
in the economy, as I mentioned.

13:15.886 --> 13:17.786 align:middle
And, third, we're
continuing asset prices

13:18.696 --> 13:21.326 align:middle
in coming months at-at a-at
a relatively high level.

13:22.086 --> 13:24.856 align:middle
So for all those reasons,

13:25.246 --> 13:29.886 align:middle
we feel like policy is
now in a good place.

13:30.026 --> 13:32.566 align:middle
So as we look ahead, we see
that the path for the-ahead

13:32.566 --> 13:35.216 align:middle
for the economy is highly
uncertain and continues

13:35.216 --> 13:36.996 align:middle
to depend to a really
significant degree

13:37.466 --> 13:38.596 align:middle
on the path of the pandemic.

13:39.036 --> 13:42.706 align:middle
So, at this meeting, what
we did was, as I mentioned,

13:42.856 --> 13:47.806 align:middle
we-we looked in some
depth at forward guidance

13:47.806 --> 13:50.416 align:middle
and asset purchases and
looked carefully at those.

13:50.716 --> 13:53.586 align:middle
We also received a briefing
on the historical experience

13:53.586 --> 13:54.816 align:middle
with yield curve control.

13:55.676 --> 13:58.106 align:middle
And we'll continue those
discussions in upcoming meetings

13:58.106 --> 14:00.676 align:middle
and evaluate our stance
and communications as more

14:00.676 --> 14:03.236 align:middle
and portion-more-more
information about the trajectory

14:03.376 --> 14:05.956 align:middle
of the economy becomes
available.

14:07.126 --> 14:10.906 align:middle
I-I would just say, in terms
of-of what we're looking for,

14:11.496 --> 14:15.176 align:middle
we-we expect to get a
better understanding

14:15.176 --> 14:17.326 align:middle
of the economy's
trajectory and, particularly,

14:17.326 --> 14:21.646 align:middle
how we should best deploy those
tools to achieve those goals.

14:21.676 --> 14:24.586 align:middle
So that's really what we're
looking to achieve, and,

14:24.586 --> 14:27.306 align:middle
as you can see, we're-we're
actively at work on that.

14:28.296 --> 14:29.666 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:29.666 --> 14:31.066 align:middle
Okay. Next, Jeanna.

14:31.066 --> 14:31.886 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

14:33.156 --> 14:35.456 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

14:35.456 --> 14:42.196 align:middle
Thank you so much for
taking our questions.

14:42.196 --> 14:45.536 align:middle
I'm-I'm curious about
your inflation forecasts,

14:45.536 --> 14:48.686 align:middle
which are-are pretty low
across the forecast horizon.

14:48.686 --> 14:52.216 align:middle
I guess just given how low
you see inflation being

14:52.216 --> 14:55.776 align:middle
over the coming years, why
is policy appropriate now,

14:55.776 --> 14:57.976 align:middle
and why not just kind of throw
everything you can-you know,

14:57.976 --> 15:01.046 align:middle
everything and the kitchen
sink at it currently?

15:01.046 --> 15:02.636 align:middle
And I guess if you could
just talk a little bit

15:02.636 --> 15:04.886 align:middle
about what urgency you see
in-in returning it back

15:04.886 --> 15:08.436 align:middle
to that 2 percent target.

15:08.436 --> 15:08.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:08.776 --> 15:10.096 align:middle
Our current policy
stance is appropriate.

15:10.096 --> 15:13.876 align:middle
And remember, we're using-we're
using our-our emergency 13(3)

15:13.876 --> 15:15.746 align:middle
lending tools to an
unprecedented extent.

15:16.376 --> 15:18.236 align:middle
We have asset purchases,
and we've now said

15:18.236 --> 15:19.856 align:middle
that we won't go
any lower than this,

15:20.236 --> 15:23.016 align:middle
and-but-but that we're prepared
to adjust as appropriate,

15:23.216 --> 15:24.966 align:middle
and rates are at the
effective lower bound.

15:24.966 --> 15:28.826 align:middle
So we have all of our tools
in-in use in a strong way.

15:28.826 --> 15:30.406 align:middle
And so what we're-what
we're waiting

15:30.406 --> 15:32.686 align:middle
for is to-is to learn more.

15:32.686 --> 15:35.676 align:middle
I-I think, actually, if you look
at the May employment report,

15:35.676 --> 15:39.576 align:middle
it's a pretty good-probably
the biggest data surprise

15:39.576 --> 15:41.036 align:middle
that anybody can remember.

15:41.506 --> 15:43.086 align:middle
It's a pretty good illustration

15:43.086 --> 15:44.866 align:middle
of just how uncertain
these times are.

15:45.426 --> 15:46.486 align:middle
The economy is reopening.

15:46.486 --> 15:49.796 align:middle
We're going to learn a
whole lot about the-the path

15:49.796 --> 15:52.576 align:middle
of the economy in the
next-in coming months.

15:52.576 --> 15:54.906 align:middle
So that's really what
we're-what we're looking for.

15:54.906 --> 15:55.896 align:middle
In terms of inflation,

15:57.156 --> 16:01.756 align:middle
you'll know that we-we
had a 128-month expansion,

16:02.396 --> 16:06.986 align:middle
and we never did quite get
inflation back to 2 percent

16:06.986 --> 16:09.046 align:middle
on a symmetric, sustained basis.

16:09.046 --> 16:11.656 align:middle
We got close for the
last couple of years,

16:11.686 --> 16:13.056 align:middle
but we never did
quite get there.

16:13.056 --> 16:15.966 align:middle
So I think we have to be
humble about our ability

16:15.966 --> 16:17.876 align:middle
to move inflation
up and particularly

16:17.876 --> 16:22.726 align:middle
when unemployment is-is going
to be above most estimates

16:22.726 --> 16:25.786 align:middle
of the natural rate
for-certainly above the median

16:25.786 --> 16:29.196 align:middle
in our-in our-in our SEP well

16:29.196 --> 16:31.666 align:middle
through the end of-past
the end of 2022.

16:32.376 --> 16:34.496 align:middle
So I do-I think we're
in the right place now.

16:34.496 --> 16:36.986 align:middle
We-we are looking carefully

16:36.986 --> 16:40.626 align:middle
at what the-as we learn more
and-and better understand

16:41.436 --> 16:45.326 align:middle
the-the path of the economy,
we will be assessing,

16:45.326 --> 16:48.646 align:middle
what's the best way to
deploy all of our tools

16:49.386 --> 16:51.486 align:middle
to achieve our goals in
the best possible way?

16:51.486 --> 16:52.586 align:middle
And I'll-I'll just say again

16:52.586 --> 16:55.856 align:middle
that we are-the-the May
employment report, of course,

16:55.856 --> 16:58.216 align:middle
was a-was a welcome
surprise, very pleased.

16:58.216 --> 16:59.496 align:middle
We hope we get many
more like it.

16:59.546 --> 17:01.556 align:middle
But I think we have
to be honest.

17:01.556 --> 17:02.656 align:middle
It's a-it's a long road.

17:02.656 --> 17:05.056 align:middle
It's-it's, depending
on how you count it,

17:05.466 --> 17:08.206 align:middle
well more than 20 million people
displaced in the labor market.

17:08.206 --> 17:09.606 align:middle
It's going to take some time.

17:10.136 --> 17:15.036 align:middle
And we are going to be deploying
our tools-all of our tools,

17:15.176 --> 17:19.606 align:middle
to their full extent, in
pursuit of that-of those goals,

17:19.926 --> 17:20.906 align:middle
however long it takes.

17:22.506 --> 17:23.566 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:29.366 --> 17:30.476 align:middle
Okay. Steve Liesman.

17:30.476 --> 17:31.536 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

17:32.926 --> 17:34.966 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

17:35.186 --> 17:38.506 align:middle
I think I understand
how the Fed might react

17:38.506 --> 17:40.986 align:middle
in the event things come
out worse than expected.

17:41.316 --> 17:44.656 align:middle
I think I don't understand how
you might react if things come

17:44.656 --> 17:46.556 align:middle
out better than expected-kind
of a variant

17:46.556 --> 17:48.366 align:middle
on these other questions.

17:48.546 --> 17:52.136 align:middle
How firm is the commitment
to lower interest rates?

17:52.206 --> 17:55.236 align:middle
And if things end up
better, do you stick

17:55.236 --> 17:58.346 align:middle
with these low interest rates,
as you-as you projected them?

17:58.716 --> 18:02.196 align:middle
And-or is it specific
points that you have

18:02.196 --> 18:03.776 align:middle
in mind-for example,
unemployment

18:03.776 --> 18:07.536 align:middle
or inflation-that is
animating where you might go?

18:07.536 --> 18:09.266 align:middle
And what are those
numbers that you're looking

18:09.266 --> 18:13.406 align:middle
for that might cause you
to change about the policy

18:13.406 --> 18:14.896 align:middle
as it now is projected?

18:14.896 --> 18:15.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:15.116 --> 18:17.126 align:middle
I would start with-that's
where I would start.

18:17.126 --> 18:19.336 align:middle
I would say, you know, we
were-we just had a period

18:19.336 --> 18:20.626 align:middle
of unemployment, as you know,

18:20.626 --> 18:23.796 align:middle
that was in the-well
below 4 percent,

18:23.796 --> 18:26.686 align:middle
hanging around 3½ percent,
that lasted two years.

18:26.956 --> 18:29.326 align:middle
And during that period
of time, we saw a lot

18:29.326 --> 18:31.496 align:middle
of great things happening
in the labor market,

18:31.676 --> 18:33.096 align:middle
things that we'd
love to get back to.

18:33.586 --> 18:35.836 align:middle
We didn't see any problems
with price inflation.

18:36.076 --> 18:37.956 align:middle
Price inflation didn't
really react much.

18:37.956 --> 18:41.566 align:middle
So, I would say, you
know, we'd be looking

18:41.566 --> 18:43.906 align:middle
to get inflation back up,
and we-we'd be prepared

18:43.906 --> 18:48.236 align:middle
to tolerate pretty low-welcome,
in fact, not tolerate

18:48.236 --> 18:52.236 align:middle
but welcome very low readings
on-on unemployment just based

18:52.236 --> 18:56.316 align:middle
on what we-what we saw in the
last-in the last expansion.

18:56.496 --> 19:00.436 align:middle
So, you know, we're-we're not
thinking about raising rates.

19:00.436 --> 19:02.696 align:middle
We're not even thinking about
thinking about raising rates.

19:03.266 --> 19:03.956 align:middle
So what we're thinking

19:03.956 --> 19:07.866 align:middle
about is-is providing
support for this economy.

19:07.866 --> 19:09.686 align:middle
We do think this is
going to take some time.

19:10.406 --> 19:13.096 align:middle
I think most forecasters
believe that.

19:13.556 --> 19:16.416 align:middle
It would be great if-if we
got a whole bunch more months

19:16.416 --> 19:18.826 align:middle
of job creation like that.

19:18.826 --> 19:20.876 align:middle
Not-notwithstanding
that, as I mentioned,

19:20.876 --> 19:23.156 align:middle
there are-there are just a lot
of people that are unemployed.

19:23.156 --> 19:24.406 align:middle
And it seems quite likely

19:24.406 --> 19:28.266 align:middle
that there will be a significant
group at the end of-even-even

19:28.266 --> 19:29.926 align:middle
after a lot of strong
job growth,

19:29.926 --> 19:31.486 align:middle
that'll still be
struggling to find jobs,

19:31.486 --> 19:36.686 align:middle
and we'll still be providing
strong accommodation for that.

19:36.876 --> 19:39.576 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:40.296 --> 19:43.116 align:middle
Okay, thank you.

19:43.116 --> 19:46.276 align:middle
James. JAMES POLITI.

19:46.486 --> 19:48.436 align:middle
Thanks very much, Chair Powell.

19:48.436 --> 19:50.696 align:middle
There have been plenty
of comparisons

19:50.696 --> 19:54.116 align:middle
with the Great Depression of the
1930s since the crisis began.

19:54.236 --> 19:58.316 align:middle
I mean, is that scenario sort of
got[ten] more dire as data comes

19:58.316 --> 20:01.746 align:middle
out and we're looking
at a more traditional

20:01.746 --> 20:04.176 align:middle
and sudden recession?

20:04.176 --> 20:05.816 align:middle
And are you at all concerned

20:05.816 --> 20:09.376 align:middle
that the performance-the strong
performance in the stock market

20:09.376 --> 20:12.406 align:middle
in the last few weeks
is disconnected

20:12.576 --> 20:15.866 align:middle
from economic reality?

20:15.866 --> 20:16.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:16.126 --> 20:18.676 align:middle
I don't think that the-that
the Great Depression is a good

20:18.676 --> 20:22.106 align:middle
example or a likely
outcome or a model

20:22.106 --> 20:24.446 align:middle
for what's happening
here at all.

20:24.446 --> 20:25.546 align:middle
I really don't.

20:25.546 --> 20:28.346 align:middle
And there-there are just so
many fundamental differences.

20:28.686 --> 20:31.076 align:middle
First, the government
response has been

20:31.076 --> 20:32.766 align:middle
so fast and so forceful.

20:33.416 --> 20:34.776 align:middle
The origin was quite different.

20:34.836 --> 20:39.276 align:middle
This was a very-an economy
that was in a healthy place.

20:39.686 --> 20:42.186 align:middle
Of course, every economy
has longer-run challenges,

20:42.186 --> 20:44.446 align:middle
not-and that-that includes
our economy, notwithstanding

20:44.446 --> 20:48.416 align:middle
that 50-year low in unemployment
and the longest expansion

20:48.416 --> 20:50.596 align:middle
in our history and every reason
to think it could continue.

20:50.596 --> 20:54.176 align:middle
So that's different from what
was happening around the time

20:54.176 --> 20:56.466 align:middle
of the Great-the
Depression started.

20:56.466 --> 20:58.746 align:middle
The financial system this
time was in-was in-was

20:59.176 --> 21:01.416 align:middle
in very good shape,
much better capitalized.

21:01.416 --> 21:04.426 align:middle
So, you know, it's just
not the right model.

21:04.426 --> 21:05.766 align:middle
I would say, we're learning.

21:05.886 --> 21:07.266 align:middle
You know, every month
that passes,

21:07.266 --> 21:08.946 align:middle
we're seeing more,
we're learning more.

21:08.946 --> 21:12.156 align:middle
And I think particularly the
next few months will be very

21:12.156 --> 21:14.866 align:middle
important in learning what
the-what the real story will be,

21:15.386 --> 21:19.356 align:middle
because we'll see the-the
significant incoming data

21:19.356 --> 21:20.746 align:middle
about the opening

21:20.746 --> 21:22.536 align:middle
of the economy-the
reopening of the economy.

21:23.026 --> 21:24.186 align:middle
You know-and I would say,

21:24.186 --> 21:28.526 align:middle
assuming that the-that
the-that the disease remains

21:28.526 --> 21:30.926 align:middle
or becomes pretty
much under control,

21:31.716 --> 21:36.436 align:middle
I think that what you see is
a-is a very weak second quarter,

21:36.436 --> 21:39.336 align:middle
historically weak, and
then an-an expansion

21:39.336 --> 21:41.166 align:middle
that builds momentum over time.

21:41.526 --> 21:44.306 align:middle
People will adjust, probably
a little bit gradually,

21:44.306 --> 21:45.656 align:middle
to some of the activities

21:46.476 --> 21:48.576 align:middle
that would-that involve
getting together

21:48.576 --> 21:50.786 align:middle
in small-in large
groups in close quarters.

21:51.206 --> 21:54.346 align:middle
Those will be the harder parts
of the economy to recover.

21:54.346 --> 21:57.636 align:middle
But, ultimately, we do see
a full recovery over time.

21:57.796 --> 22:00.546 align:middle
And that's really
what, you know,

22:00.546 --> 22:02.576 align:middle
what I think I'm-I'm
personally seeing.

22:02.576 --> 22:05.626 align:middle
And you could see significant
job growth in-in coming months

22:05.626 --> 22:08.476 align:middle
as people return to their
jobs, but you're still going

22:08.476 --> 22:10.786 align:middle
to face probably
an extended period

22:10.786 --> 22:13.806 align:middle
where it will be difficult
for many people to find work.

22:13.806 --> 22:17.466 align:middle
And for-and that's what you
see in-in-in, really, many,

22:17.466 --> 22:18.726 align:middle
many forecasts at this point.

22:18.726 --> 22:20.346 align:middle
That doesn't mean it's
right, but that's sort

22:20.346 --> 22:21.886 align:middle
of a-a broad expectation,

22:21.886 --> 22:23.546 align:middle
certainly not the
Depression forecast.

22:24.446 --> 22:25.046 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:26.486 --> 22:27.506 align:middle
Thank you.

22:27.636 --> 22:30.396 align:middle
Chris from the Associated Press.

22:30.396 --> 22:32.426 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

22:32.426 --> 22:37.586 align:middle
Hi. Thank you for
taking our questions.

22:38.176 --> 22:41.676 align:middle
Well, in the projections you
have-you didn't change the-the

22:41.676 --> 22:44.396 align:middle
Fed policymakers didn't
change their-the forecast

22:44.396 --> 22:49.256 align:middle
for long-run unemployment, so
that suggests that you-you know,

22:49.486 --> 22:52.536 align:middle
the-that all of you so far
don't see necessarily prospects

22:52.536 --> 22:54.306 align:middle
for long-term damage.

22:54.306 --> 22:58.206 align:middle
But still, what kind of data are
you looking at here to gauge,

22:58.206 --> 23:00.466 align:middle
you know, the potential
for longer-term hits

23:00.466 --> 23:04.156 align:middle
to the economy even as we
have some of these-even

23:04.156 --> 23:06.186 align:middle
as we have something
like the May jobs report,

23:06.186 --> 23:09.256 align:middle
you could still have, at
the same time, people return

23:09.256 --> 23:10.176 align:middle
from temporary layoffs,

23:10.176 --> 23:13.136 align:middle
there could still be
permanent job losses?

23:13.136 --> 23:16.036 align:middle
So what kind of data are
you looking at to gauge

23:16.036 --> 23:19.086 align:middle
that potential impact, and what
is-what is it telling us so far?

23:19.086 --> 23:19.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:19.296 --> 23:20.346 align:middle
Chris, this is a
really important risk.

23:20.346 --> 23:22.856 align:middle
And I think it's-it's not the
risk for the next few months,

23:22.856 --> 23:26.716 align:middle
but it's the risk over
time of-of lasting damage

23:26.716 --> 23:29.126 align:middle
to the-to these-you know,
to the productive capacity

23:29.126 --> 23:31.536 align:middle
of the United States,
typically in two forms.

23:31.536 --> 23:34.166 align:middle
One, through extended
periods of unemployment,

23:34.166 --> 23:36.066 align:middle
people lose contact
with the labor force.

23:36.386 --> 23:39.416 align:middle
They get out of touch with
the skills and-that they need,

23:39.416 --> 23:41.236 align:middle
and-and they have a hard
time getting back in,

23:41.236 --> 23:44.576 align:middle
and that drives that-that,
you know, it's very damaging

23:44.576 --> 23:46.736 align:middle
to people's lives and
their working lives.

23:47.256 --> 23:49.086 align:middle
And it also just lowers,

23:49.186 --> 23:52.676 align:middle
in a way-it can-it can
increase the-you know,

23:52.676 --> 23:53.886 align:middle
the unemployment rate,

23:53.886 --> 23:56.106 align:middle
but it can also lower the
labor force participation rate,

23:56.106 --> 23:58.676 align:middle
which is almost worse, in a
way, to have people dropping

23:58.676 --> 24:00.046 align:middle
out of the labor force
where we need them

24:00.046 --> 24:01.406 align:middle
in the-in the labor
force working.

24:01.986 --> 24:03.866 align:middle
The other piece of it
is just businesses.

24:04.226 --> 24:06.956 align:middle
You know, a shock like
this that just comes in,

24:06.956 --> 24:08.336 align:middle
it's like a natural disaster.

24:08.756 --> 24:11.206 align:middle
You wouldn't want a lot of
perfectly good businesses,

24:11.206 --> 24:13.156 align:middle
particularly the smaller
and medium-sized businesses

24:13.156 --> 24:14.696 align:middle
that may not have
a lot of resources

24:14.696 --> 24:18.926 align:middle
to sustain them to-you know, to
go out of business permanently

24:19.476 --> 24:22.306 align:middle
in a situation like
that-like this where, really,

24:22.366 --> 24:23.876 align:middle
there was no reason for it.

24:23.876 --> 24:26.406 align:middle
Of course, you know, businesses
are going to go in and out.

24:26.406 --> 24:27.596 align:middle
They're going to
fail all the time,

24:27.596 --> 24:29.256 align:middle
and that's a healthy
thing in capitalism.

24:29.256 --> 24:30.506 align:middle
That's something
that has to happen.

24:30.876 --> 24:31.676 align:middle
But this is different.

24:31.676 --> 24:33.806 align:middle
This is a way-potentially-so
those are the things we've been

24:33.806 --> 24:34.526 align:middle
worried about.

24:34.936 --> 24:35.786 align:middle
We didn't change.

24:36.096 --> 24:37.296 align:middle
You-you're right, though.

24:37.296 --> 24:40.786 align:middle
We didn't change our longer-run
estimate of-of potential growth

24:40.886 --> 24:42.436 align:middle
or of the unemployment rate.

24:42.986 --> 24:44.946 align:middle
And that's-I would
say, in my thinking,

24:44.946 --> 24:47.306 align:middle
the reason I didn't
change mine is

24:47.306 --> 24:50.366 align:middle
that I still-I-I think we
can avoid that or avoid much

24:50.366 --> 24:51.996 align:middle
of that, most of that even.

24:52.026 --> 24:55.916 align:middle
And-and we do that with
measures that-that, you know,

24:55.946 --> 25:01.006 align:middle
keep people in their homes;
that-that support hiring;

25:01.006 --> 25:03.416 align:middle
that support growth;
that avoid unnecessary,

25:03.416 --> 25:05.516 align:middle
avoidable business insolvencies.

25:05.516 --> 25:07.296 align:middle
And that's all the things
that we're trying to do.

25:07.786 --> 25:10.456 align:middle
Basically, if you look at
what's happened, as I mentioned,

25:10.456 --> 25:12.446 align:middle
there's something-somewhere
short

25:12.446 --> 25:14.646 align:middle
of 25 million people
have been displaced,

25:14.646 --> 25:16.616 align:middle
even after the good
May employment report.

25:16.776 --> 25:18.866 align:middle
What we're trying to do
is create an environment

25:18.866 --> 25:21.446 align:middle
in which they have the best
chance either to go back

25:21.446 --> 25:23.736 align:middle
to their old job or
to get a new job.

25:23.896 --> 25:26.766 align:middle
That's-that's kind of the most
important part of this exercise.

25:26.766 --> 25:29.846 align:middle
And maybe these-it's
probably hopeful at this point

25:29.846 --> 25:32.576 align:middle
to say that-that we won't
have longer-run damage

25:32.576 --> 25:34.506 align:middle
to the economy, and these
numbers won't change.

25:35.796 --> 25:37.856 align:middle
So-but I think it's
way too early

25:37.856 --> 25:40.636 align:middle
to be-to be changing
the longer run-see,

25:40.636 --> 25:42.646 align:middle
these are-these are not meant
to be short-run numbers.

25:42.646 --> 25:44.526 align:middle
They're called-they're
longer-run assessments,

25:44.526 --> 25:47.156 align:middle
so I-I would-I have
not changed mine,

25:47.156 --> 25:50.556 align:middle
and I'm hopeful that-that
I won't have to change it.

25:50.556 --> 25:52.426 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:54.396 --> 25:54.636 align:middle
Heather Long.

25:57.576 --> 25:58.826 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

26:02.786 --> 26:05.296 align:middle
Hi. Good afternoon,
Chair Powell.

26:05.296 --> 26:06.966 align:middle
I'm struggling with two things

26:06.966 --> 26:09.196 align:middle
that I hope you can
provide some clarity on.

26:09.196 --> 26:12.296 align:middle
The first is the ongoing
bond buying program.

26:12.296 --> 26:16.616 align:middle
You say that it's needed to
continue the smooth functioning

26:16.616 --> 26:19.856 align:middle
of markets, but I guess most of
us aren't really seeing a lot

26:19.856 --> 26:22.286 align:middle
of instability in
markets right now.

26:22.286 --> 26:24.026 align:middle
So if you could kind
of give us some clarity

26:24.026 --> 26:26.996 align:middle
of what you're seeing that
needs to continue to be smooth

26:26.996 --> 26:28.636 align:middle
at that level and that pace.

26:29.166 --> 26:32.396 align:middle
Second is, you know, you were
just talking about Fed concerns

26:32.396 --> 26:35.606 align:middle
about small and medium-sized
companies going

26:35.606 --> 26:37.486 align:middle
out of business during this.

26:37.526 --> 26:40.896 align:middle
And, I guess, you know, the Main
Street Lending Program still

26:40.896 --> 26:41.976 align:middle
isn't running yet.

26:41.976 --> 26:44.856 align:middle
When do you expect loans
to start happening?

26:44.856 --> 26:48.476 align:middle
And do you think the two-month
delay has-has hurt the chances

26:48.476 --> 26:50.096 align:middle
of some companies surviving?

26:50.986 --> 26:51.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:51.366 --> 26:57.226 align:middle
There have been gains
in market function,

26:57.936 --> 27:01.696 align:middle
although not fully back to
where you would say they were,

27:01.836 --> 27:04.366 align:middle
for example, in-in February,
before the pandemic arrived.

27:04.826 --> 27:07.646 align:middle
We don't take those gains
for granted, though.

27:07.646 --> 27:10.306 align:middle
This is a-this is a
highly fluid situation,

27:10.856 --> 27:12.686 align:middle
and we're-we're not
taking those for granted.

27:12.686 --> 27:15.596 align:middle
And, in addition, as I pointed
out in my-in my statement,

27:16.096 --> 27:20.166 align:middle
those purchases are clearly
also supporting highly

27:20.166 --> 27:23.406 align:middle
accommodative-or accommodative
financial conditions,

27:23.406 --> 27:26.776 align:middle
and that's-that's a good thing,
so that's why we're doing that.

27:26.956 --> 27:29.716 align:middle
Turning quickly to Main
Street, so I would say

27:29.716 --> 27:32.676 align:middle
that the thing-what we've
done on Main Street,

27:32.926 --> 27:36.836 align:middle
I think to a greater degree,
is, we've listened to feedback.

27:36.836 --> 27:39.936 align:middle
So we've been out repeatedly
for feedback in trying

27:39.936 --> 27:43.066 align:middle
to create a-a much more
difficult product than,

27:43.066 --> 27:44.306 align:middle
really, the other facilities.

27:44.896 --> 27:47.286 align:middle
And I think this-this last set

27:47.286 --> 27:49.396 align:middle
of changes we made have
actually been very positive

27:49.396 --> 27:50.056 align:middle
for the facility.

27:50.056 --> 27:54.746 align:middle
I think it's going to be better
able to achieve its goals, and,

27:55.156 --> 27:57.526 align:middle
you know, so we've used
the time well, I think.

27:57.526 --> 28:00.026 align:middle
And that-we are now in the-in
the-in the final run-up

28:00.056 --> 28:01.476 align:middle
to starting the facility.

28:01.856 --> 28:05.666 align:middle
What we did, as you saw
earlier-I guess it's early this

28:05.666 --> 28:07.166 align:middle
week or late, whenever we did it

28:07.166 --> 28:11.766 align:middle
in the last few days-was we
lowered the minimum loan size

28:11.766 --> 28:13.366 align:middle
and we increased the
maximum loan size.

28:13.366 --> 28:14.596 align:middle
But, I think, even
more importantly,

28:14.596 --> 28:16.336 align:middle
we lengthened the
maturity and we stretched

28:16.336 --> 28:18.656 align:middle
out the repayment
schedule significantly.

28:18.656 --> 28:23.056 align:middle
So borrowers will get a-a
two-year delay until they have

28:23.056 --> 28:25.246 align:middle
to make any principal
payments-repayments

28:25.246 --> 28:26.736 align:middle
and a one-year delay
on interest.

28:26.736 --> 28:30.596 align:middle
So we had been hearing from
both borrowers and lenders

28:31.016 --> 28:32.356 align:middle
that these would
be very helpful.

28:32.386 --> 28:33.506 align:middle
We made the changes.

28:33.506 --> 28:35.076 align:middle
We're putting them
through the facility.

28:35.076 --> 28:37.176 align:middle
The next step will be
to register lenders.

28:37.606 --> 28:40.806 align:middle
At that time, loans
can begin to be made.

28:41.046 --> 28:43.546 align:middle
Shortly after that, the
facility itself will be up,

28:43.546 --> 28:47.466 align:middle
and those facilities can
be-those loans can be sold-95

28:47.466 --> 28:48.146 align:middle
percent of the loans.

28:48.146 --> 28:50.086 align:middle
And it's 95 percent
across the board now

28:50.086 --> 28:52.506 align:middle
in the Main Street
facility can be sold.

28:52.506 --> 28:54.566 align:middle
So all of that should
happen quickly now.

28:54.666 --> 28:58.036 align:middle
And I do think this has-this
has been a challenging project,

28:58.036 --> 29:00.816 align:middle
but I think we've come
to a-to a better place.

29:00.816 --> 29:02.596 align:middle
And, by the way, we're-we're
going to be prepared

29:02.596 --> 29:04.316 align:middle
to adapt further, if we need to.

29:04.316 --> 29:06.146 align:middle
And-and that's true of
all of our facilities.

29:06.176 --> 29:07.026 align:middle
These are unique.

29:07.686 --> 29:09.586 align:middle
There's no-there's
no playbook here.

29:09.586 --> 29:13.476 align:middle
You-you have to draw this
up and then try it out.

29:13.476 --> 29:16.766 align:middle
And we've-we've been, you
know, very willing to adapt

29:16.766 --> 29:20.296 align:middle
and will continue to be.

29:20.296 --> 29:25.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:25.126 --> 29:27.526 align:middle
Okay, Matt from Bloomberg.

29:27.526 --> 29:30.556 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

29:30.556 --> 29:31.166 align:middle
Hello, Chair Powell.

29:31.166 --> 29:33.026 align:middle
This is Matthew Boesler
with Bloomberg News.

29:33.026 --> 29:35.806 align:middle
I wanted to ask you about
regulatory forbearance.

29:35.806 --> 29:38.846 align:middle
There were some piecemeal
forbearance measures put

29:38.846 --> 29:41.086 align:middle
into place in various
jurisdictions and also

29:41.086 --> 29:43.976 align:middle
to a slice of the mortgage
market to prevent banks

29:43.976 --> 29:47.456 align:middle
from foreclosing on such debts
amid the widespread financial

29:47.456 --> 29:49.156 align:middle
hardships caused
by the pandemic.

29:49.156 --> 29:52.486 align:middle
And with many of those set to
expire soon and many businesses

29:52.486 --> 29:55.306 align:middle
and households still in dire
financial straits, I'm wondering

29:55.306 --> 29:58.306 align:middle
if any consideration is
being given at the Fed

29:58.306 --> 30:01.636 align:middle
to more comprehensive
regulatory forbearance measures

30:01.636 --> 30:04.956 align:middle
or other regulatory measures to
prevent businesses from going

30:04.956 --> 30:07.256 align:middle
under just because they
can't meet those payments.

30:07.376 --> 30:09.256 align:middle
There have, of course,
been some tweaks

30:09.256 --> 30:12.036 align:middle
to the Paycheck Protection
Program on the fiscal side

30:12.036 --> 30:14.056 align:middle
to address this, but
I wanted to ask you

30:14.056 --> 30:15.346 align:middle
about things you
might be able to do

30:15.346 --> 30:17.156 align:middle
on the regulatory side as well.

30:17.156 --> 30:17.756 align:middle
Thank you.

30:18.956 --> 30:19.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:19.476 --> 30:24.816 align:middle
We can-we can make changes to
bank regulation and supervision.

30:24.816 --> 30:28.736 align:middle
I don't know that we have
the ability to make changes,

30:28.736 --> 30:30.556 align:middle
for example, in mortgage
payments,

30:30.556 --> 30:32.386 align:middle
if that's what you're thinking
of, or credit card payments.

30:32.416 --> 30:35.206 align:middle
That's really-that's something
that could be legislative,

30:35.206 --> 30:37.676 align:middle
or it could just be what
the-what the banks themselves

30:37.676 --> 30:38.096 align:middle
are doing.

30:38.096 --> 30:40.086 align:middle
There's been a tremendous amount
of forbearance on the part

30:40.086 --> 30:42.976 align:middle
of the banks, and I guess
our role there would be

30:42.976 --> 30:45.076 align:middle
to encourage it, but
those are not decisions

30:45.076 --> 30:47.796 align:middle
that we hold any legal authority
to make, and we-by the way,

30:47.796 --> 30:49.796 align:middle
we have encouraged
those decisions.

30:49.956 --> 30:51.716 align:middle
I hope that's responsive
to your question.

30:52.186 --> 30:53.746 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:55.986 --> 31:00.136 align:middle
Okay. Scott Horsley, NPR.

31:00.136 --> 31:01.176 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

31:01.176 --> 31:06.136 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

31:07.766 --> 31:10.886 align:middle
I know you're loathe to weigh
in too heavily on fiscal policy,

31:10.886 --> 31:14.716 align:middle
but given your forecast for
elevated unemployment rates,

31:14.776 --> 31:16.186 align:middle
do you think it's important

31:16.186 --> 31:19.316 align:middle
that Congress extend
the $600 a week extra

31:19.316 --> 31:21.966 align:middle
unemployment benefits?

31:21.966 --> 31:22.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:22.946 --> 31:28.466 align:middle
I think we try to keep our
comments on fiscal policy

31:28.466 --> 31:32.406 align:middle
at a high level, and I'll-I'll
come to your specific question,

31:32.486 --> 31:37.016 align:middle
but I would just say this: This
is the biggest economic shock

31:37.706 --> 31:40.026 align:middle
in the U.S. and in the world,
really, in living memory.

31:40.586 --> 31:44.576 align:middle
We went from the lowest level of
employ-unemployment in 50 years

31:44.576 --> 31:47.526 align:middle
to the highest level
in close to 90 years,

31:47.526 --> 31:48.806 align:middle
and we did it in two months.

31:49.626 --> 31:50.376 align:middle
Extraordinary.

31:50.606 --> 31:53.496 align:middle
And, appropriately, the response

31:53.496 --> 31:58.126 align:middle
from fiscal authorities
has been large, forceful,

31:58.656 --> 32:02.296 align:middle
and very quick by-by the
standards of these things.

32:02.996 --> 32:06.066 align:middle
Roughly $3 trillion
Congress has authorized,

32:06.066 --> 32:09.026 align:middle
and that's benefiting
households; laid-off workers;

32:09.026 --> 32:11.566 align:middle
small, medium, and large
businesses; hospitals;

32:11.566 --> 32:14.256 align:middle
state and local governments-14
percent of GDP.

32:14.256 --> 32:18.146 align:middle
It is-it's in a class by itself,
in terms of both the size

32:18.146 --> 32:21.036 align:middle
and the speed of it, and
it's also pretty innovative.

32:21.036 --> 32:21.966 align:middle
You know, the-both the PPP

32:21.966 --> 32:25.446 align:middle
and the unemployment
insurance are quite innovative

32:25.446 --> 32:28.666 align:middle
in the American context,
and there were difficulties

32:28.666 --> 32:32.186 align:middle
in implementing it, but that's
really a function of-of their

32:32.186 --> 32:33.086 align:middle
novelty, I think.

32:33.636 --> 32:35.696 align:middle
Those programs-by
the way, let me add:

32:35.696 --> 32:38.726 align:middle
The Fed also-we both
innovated and acted forcefully,

32:38.726 --> 32:41.646 align:middle
progressively, proactively,
and aggressively as well.

32:41.646 --> 32:42.596 align:middle
If you put those together,

32:43.056 --> 32:44.706 align:middle
all of that is making
a difference now.

32:45.186 --> 32:46.616 align:middle
You look at the income data.

32:46.716 --> 32:48.476 align:middle
The expanded unemployment
insurance

32:48.476 --> 32:51.066 align:middle
and also the stimulus
checks have gone a long way

32:51.066 --> 32:53.646 align:middle
to replacing lost income
from-from job loss.

32:54.126 --> 32:55.796 align:middle
I think you're seeing it
in the job market data.

32:56.276 --> 33:00.416 align:middle
Many are giving the PPP
credit on that front and-and

33:00.416 --> 33:02.276 align:middle
in keeping small
businesses going.

33:02.276 --> 33:06.226 align:middle
So it's-so far, it's
a good response,

33:06.226 --> 33:07.836 align:middle
and it's having-it's
having a big effect.

33:07.836 --> 33:09.376 align:middle
You-you get to-the question is,

33:09.866 --> 33:11.806 align:middle
it's big-everyone can
see that it's big.

33:11.806 --> 33:13.826 align:middle
Is it going to be big enough?

33:13.826 --> 33:15.216 align:middle
And that-that is the question.

33:15.216 --> 33:18.226 align:middle
And-and the question
for-for-the question

33:18.226 --> 33:21.186 align:middle
that I've been concerned
about, really, is this issue

33:21.186 --> 33:22.776 align:middle
of longer-run damage
to the economy.

33:23.466 --> 33:26.156 align:middle
We're-we're-we're doing
a fair job of getting

33:26.156 --> 33:28.186 align:middle
through these first few
months, more than a fair job.

33:28.186 --> 33:30.676 align:middle
The question, though,
is, that group of people

33:31.006 --> 33:33.586 align:middle
who won't be able to go back to
work quickly, what about them?

33:33.586 --> 33:37.716 align:middle
And that could be many
millions of people, you know,

33:37.716 --> 33:39.576 align:middle
parts-who worked in
parts of the economy

33:39.576 --> 33:41.026 align:middle
that will be the
slow ones to recover.

33:41.686 --> 33:43.906 align:middle
And, you know, we want those
people back in the labor force.

33:43.906 --> 33:45.006 align:middle
We want them getting jobs.

33:45.006 --> 33:49.176 align:middle
So-and-and they're going to
need possibly-probably will need

33:49.176 --> 33:49.756 align:middle
further support.

33:49.756 --> 33:51.946 align:middle
So I will just say this: It's
possible that we will need

33:51.946 --> 33:53.156 align:middle
to do more, and it's possible

33:53.156 --> 33:54.326 align:middle
that Congress will
need to do more.

33:54.936 --> 33:57.306 align:middle
In terms of the $600
unemployment insurance,

33:57.346 --> 34:00.706 align:middle
I wouldn't-I wouldn't try
to give Congress advice

34:00.706 --> 34:01.976 align:middle
on the specifics of that.

34:01.976 --> 34:05.406 align:middle
I-I know they're looking at-I
mean, I-I know from both talking

34:05.406 --> 34:08.536 align:middle
to people and-and reading the
papers that they're looking

34:08.536 --> 34:11.236 align:middle
at a whole bunch of different
possible approaches going

34:11.236 --> 34:13.936 align:middle
forward, and some of those
seem kind of promising.

34:14.336 --> 34:17.826 align:middle
So, you know, we're happy
to give advice if people ask

34:17.826 --> 34:21.236 align:middle
for it but-but probably
not-not publicly.

34:21.236 --> 34:21.876 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:25.016 --> 34:25.756 align:middle
Thank you.

34:25.916 --> 34:28.946 align:middle
David Gura.

34:29.096 --> 34:30.266 align:middle
DAVID GURA.

34:30.376 --> 34:32.746 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

34:32.746 --> 34:35.136 align:middle
I'm going to pick up on what
Scott Horsley was asking just a

34:35.136 --> 34:35.716 align:middle
moment ago.

34:35.716 --> 34:38.246 align:middle
You have been studious
about threading this needle

34:38.396 --> 34:40.366 align:middle
in the interviews that
you've given and speeches

34:40.366 --> 34:43.986 align:middle
and the-the colloquy that's
followed, not giving advice

34:43.986 --> 34:46.696 align:middle
to Congress but indicating that
there's likely to be a need

34:46.696 --> 34:50.636 align:middle
for more fiscal stimulus or
additional policy in the future.

34:50.876 --> 34:52.736 align:middle
So I'm going to try
a hypothetical here,

34:52.736 --> 34:54.246 align:middle
and you can rebuff
it if-if you want.

34:54.246 --> 34:55.496 align:middle
But if you were to find yourself

34:55.496 --> 34:57.076 align:middle
in a-in a senator's
office suite,

34:57.076 --> 34:59.726 align:middle
and he held up that May
unemployment report,

34:59.786 --> 35:00.636 align:middle
which you've described

35:00.636 --> 35:04.276 align:middle
as unexpectedly positive and-and
a welcome surprise, and if he

35:04.276 --> 35:06.976 align:middle
or she said to you, "Look, this
is an indication that we can put

35:06.976 --> 35:09.686 align:middle
on the breaks," I wonder
what you would say.

35:09.686 --> 35:11.966 align:middle
Your-your message throughout
this crisis has been one

35:11.966 --> 35:13.746 align:middle
of how unprecedented it is,

35:13.796 --> 35:17.146 align:middle
about how much urgency there
is in dealing with this.

35:17.146 --> 35:19.206 align:middle
Indeed, we've seen that
from the Federal Reserve.

35:19.556 --> 35:21.566 align:middle
I wonder if you worry
that that sense

35:21.566 --> 35:24.326 align:middle
of urgency isn't being matched
and-and what the consequences

35:24.326 --> 35:27.106 align:middle
of that might be, if there's a
fear that the Fed could be seen

35:27.106 --> 35:29.966 align:middle
as a Cassandra here as we look
at that unemployment report

35:29.966 --> 35:31.596 align:middle
and then through the
summer, and you have more

35:31.596 --> 35:33.766 align:middle
and more politicians
inclined just to wait

35:33.766 --> 35:36.466 align:middle
and see what happens here
in-in subsequent reports.

35:36.466 --> 35:37.906 align:middle
And a quick second
question, if I could.

35:37.906 --> 35:40.426 align:middle
You've talked a lot about
your fear of a second wave

35:40.686 --> 35:42.056 align:middle
of this disease,
and it strikes me,

35:42.056 --> 35:44.606 align:middle
there's a-a difficulty
here with definition.

35:44.606 --> 35:46.286 align:middle
You've talked about us
weathering this crisis

35:46.286 --> 35:47.606 align:middle
and getting through this storm.

35:47.606 --> 35:50.506 align:middle
It seems to me, as I
listen to the chatter coming

35:50.506 --> 35:53.416 align:middle
from the White House and from
politicians and epidemiologists,

35:53.416 --> 35:56.546 align:middle
there isn't agreement on what
"getting through this" means.

35:56.546 --> 36:00.936 align:middle
And you have an Administration
now inclined to, if not direct,

36:00.936 --> 36:03.086 align:middle
be content with states opening

36:03.086 --> 36:04.886 align:middle
up their economies
perhaps too early.

36:05.266 --> 36:07.486 align:middle
How problematic is that
from a policy perspective,

36:07.526 --> 36:09.316 align:middle
not having agreement
on what it means

36:09.316 --> 36:11.876 align:middle
to have defeated this
virus or gotten to a place

36:11.876 --> 36:13.146 align:middle
where the economy
can get through this?

36:13.146 --> 36:13.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:13.746 --> 36:16.096 align:middle
I can't offer any advice, but
I will provide a little context

36:16.096 --> 36:19.736 align:middle
for the-for-I want to be clear
about the picture-one way

36:19.736 --> 36:22.256 align:middle
to look at the picture,
really, and that is this:

36:22.256 --> 36:25.646 align:middle
You can-you can get at this
by looking at, you know,

36:25.646 --> 36:28.926 align:middle
the-the-the widest
measure of unemployment,

36:29.186 --> 36:33.906 align:middle
of labor market slack, if
you will, is the U-6 measure.

36:34.596 --> 36:36.656 align:middle
And the U-that's
the-the one we look-talk

36:36.656 --> 36:39.336 align:middle
about all the time is
called U-3, by economists.

36:39.336 --> 36:43.106 align:middle
And the-the one that has a much
broader measurement of slack

36:43.106 --> 36:44.286 align:middle
in the economy is U-6.

36:44.286 --> 36:49.526 align:middle
So in-in-in U 6, the U-6 level
of unemployment has tripled

36:49.526 --> 36:51.416 align:middle
from 7 percent to 21 percent.

36:51.946 --> 36:55.526 align:middle
That-that amounts to 22
million additional people

36:56.006 --> 36:59.636 align:middle
who are-who've-who've lost work
in the economy either by going

36:59.636 --> 37:01.286 align:middle
to part time or something
like that.

37:01.286 --> 37:02.846 align:middle
So that's in the low 20s,

37:02.846 --> 37:04.916 align:middle
and that's post the
May employment report.

37:05.536 --> 37:06.666 align:middle
You can get at it
a different way.

37:06.666 --> 37:09.536 align:middle
You can look at-at just the
regular unemployment rate.

37:09.536 --> 37:11.646 align:middle
You can take the
21 [million]-people

37:11.646 --> 37:14.556 align:middle
who have-had-are listed
as unemployed right now.

37:14.556 --> 37:17.306 align:middle
You can add more on for
the ones who were miscoded.

37:17.536 --> 37:20.266 align:middle
You can also take those
people who are suddenly

37:20.266 --> 37:21.046 align:middle
out of the labor force.

37:21.046 --> 37:24.586 align:middle
You put those together, and that
has gone up by about 24 million.

37:25.106 --> 37:27.156 align:middle
So those are two different
measures of what's happened.

37:27.586 --> 37:32.446 align:middle
So the question is, 20,
22, 24 million people-got

37:32.446 --> 37:33.416 align:middle
to get them back to work.

37:33.586 --> 37:35.216 align:middle
Somehow, we've got
to all, as a country,

37:35.216 --> 37:36.466 align:middle
get those people back to work.

37:37.126 --> 37:39.426 align:middle
They didn't do anything wrong;
this was a natural disaster,

37:39.426 --> 37:41.286 align:middle
and I-I really-I do
think the responses

37:41.286 --> 37:42.206 align:middle
so far have been great.

37:42.586 --> 37:43.926 align:middle
So that's the way I
would think about it.

37:43.926 --> 37:47.506 align:middle
And if-if you think
we-I would just,

37:47.506 --> 37:49.266 align:middle
in terms of the May
employment report,

37:49.896 --> 37:51.386 align:middle
it's-it's so nice to see.

37:51.386 --> 37:53.896 align:middle
I mean, I think what people were
thinking was that you'd start

37:53.896 --> 37:56.566 align:middle
to get those back-to-work
kind of numbers

37:56.566 --> 37:58.286 align:middle
in June and July and August.

37:58.646 --> 38:00.306 align:middle
Very few people saw them in May.

38:00.306 --> 38:02.486 align:middle
I mean, almost no one saw
that happening as early

38:02.486 --> 38:03.646 align:middle
as mid-May, but it did.

38:04.316 --> 38:07.546 align:middle
And so, you know, and
we-we don't know fully what

38:07.546 --> 38:09.336 align:middle
that means, whether it's
just a timing change

38:09.336 --> 38:11.726 align:middle
or whether it will prove
to be much more than that.

38:11.726 --> 38:13.606 align:middle
We'll-we're going to
have to wait and see.

38:13.606 --> 38:16.626 align:middle
It is a-as I mentioned
earlier, it is clear evidence

38:16.626 --> 38:21.026 align:middle
of just how uncertain things
are, and-and how humble we have

38:21.026 --> 38:23.756 align:middle
to be about our ability
to-to really have confident

38:23.756 --> 38:26.266 align:middle
predictions as opposed
to just predictions.

38:26.686 --> 38:28.226 align:middle
So, bottom line,
I would just say,

38:28.226 --> 38:30.656 align:middle
that-that-the key thing
people need to understand is

38:30.656 --> 38:33.236 align:middle
that there's just a lot of
work to do in the labor market.

38:33.766 --> 38:35.396 align:middle
We're going to stick
with this and support

38:35.396 --> 38:37.766 align:middle
that until the work is done.

38:38.376 --> 38:41.366 align:middle
That you-you can-that is
something we're going to do

38:41.366 --> 38:42.446 align:middle
with all of our tools,

38:42.966 --> 38:46.316 align:middle
and I think it may require
Congress to help as well.

38:46.526 --> 38:51.376 align:middle
It may, but that's going
to be their decision.

38:51.376 --> 38:53.596 align:middle
On-you also asked
about the second wave.

38:53.736 --> 38:57.916 align:middle
So we-you know, we have
major responsibilities

38:57.916 --> 39:01.236 align:middle
and powerful tools, but
the decision about when

39:01.236 --> 39:06.296 align:middle
to reopen the economy is one for
elected officials at the state

39:06.296 --> 39:08.976 align:middle
and local and federal level.

39:08.976 --> 39:10.866 align:middle
And, you know, if they ask,

39:11.156 --> 39:14.286 align:middle
we-we don't have any particular
expertise here at the Fed

39:14.286 --> 39:18.476 align:middle
in pandemics or coronaviruses
or anything like that.

39:18.476 --> 39:20.806 align:middle
We talk to experts,
but-you know,

39:20.806 --> 39:22.886 align:middle
so we don't have anything
special to add on that.

39:23.316 --> 39:26.746 align:middle
I would just say, it's-you
know, it's kind of self evident,

39:26.746 --> 39:30.876 align:middle
I think, that if a-if
it happens, you know,

39:31.236 --> 39:33.746 align:middle
the-the issue would be, first
of all, people's health,

39:33.746 --> 39:36.966 align:middle
but secondly, you
could see a public loss

39:36.966 --> 39:40.736 align:middle
of confidence in-in
parts of the economy

39:41.126 --> 39:43.006 align:middle
that will be already
slow to recover.

39:43.006 --> 39:45.026 align:middle
So it could hurt
the recovery even

39:45.026 --> 39:46.916 align:middle
if you don't have a
national-level pandemic,

39:46.946 --> 39:50.186 align:middle
just a-just a series
of-of local ones,

39:50.626 --> 39:53.306 align:middle
of local spikes could-could
have the effect

39:53.306 --> 39:55.706 align:middle
of undermining people's
confidence in traveling,

39:55.706 --> 39:57.996 align:middle
in restaurants and
entertainment,

39:57.996 --> 40:00.736 align:middle
anything that involves getting
people together in small groups

40:00.736 --> 40:03.466 align:middle
and feeding them or flying
them around-those things could

40:03.466 --> 40:03.776 align:middle
be hurt.

40:03.776 --> 40:06.106 align:middle
So it would not be a
positive development,

40:06.106 --> 40:09.826 align:middle
and I'll just leave it at that.

40:10.906 --> 40:11.846 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:14.496 --> 40:15.426 align:middle
Thank you.

40:15.426 --> 40:16.586 align:middle
Edward Lawrence.

40:16.586 --> 40:17.876 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

40:17.876 --> 40:19.936 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman
Powell, for the question.

40:19.936 --> 40:22.766 align:middle
On Main Street lending
facilities, you know,

40:22.766 --> 40:26.446 align:middle
in May we saw 55-at least 55
companies file for bankruptcy.

40:26.446 --> 40:29.366 align:middle
Do you think that it is
too late, first of all?

40:29.366 --> 40:31.136 align:middle
Second of all, the future

40:31.136 --> 40:34.606 align:middle
of these facilities-is this a
one-year impact to the economy

40:34.606 --> 40:37.156 align:middle
through these facilities,
or is this a multiple year?

40:37.156 --> 40:38.706 align:middle
If so, how many years?

40:39.006 --> 40:39.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:39.486 --> 40:43.126 align:middle
We have, you know,
significant interest, we think.

40:43.126 --> 40:45.546 align:middle
And also, remember,
lots and lots

40:45.546 --> 40:47.336 align:middle
of companies are
getting financed, too.

40:47.906 --> 40:49.666 align:middle
The-the banks are lending.

40:49.666 --> 40:50.686 align:middle
The markets are open.

40:50.686 --> 40:56.206 align:middle
You know, you have a-a-a much
better lending climate than-than

40:56.206 --> 40:58.786 align:middle
certainly than we had
in February and March.

40:58.786 --> 40:59.786 align:middle
We don't think it's
too late, though.

41:00.436 --> 41:03.006 align:middle
We-we-and we do expect
it to be up quite soon.

41:03.686 --> 41:06.246 align:middle
In terms of the timing of
these things, so, you know,

41:07.466 --> 41:10.586 align:middle
we'll leave them open as-we'll
leave them open for new loans

41:11.306 --> 41:12.966 align:middle
as long as they need to.

41:12.966 --> 41:16.446 align:middle
That'll be a decision we make,
of course, with our colleagues

41:16.446 --> 41:17.426 align:middle
at the Treasury Department.

41:18.166 --> 41:20.316 align:middle
And, at a certain point,
there won't be a need

41:20.316 --> 41:23.696 align:middle
for further loans, and-and then
the assets will-will be there.

41:24.426 --> 41:26.906 align:middle
And so if that's-I don't
know if that's a part

41:26.906 --> 41:28.686 align:middle
of your question too, but
I would just say that,

41:28.686 --> 41:31.726 align:middle
at that point, you know,
the-the useful role

41:31.726 --> 41:34.056 align:middle
of the-of the Federal
Reserve is probably close

41:34.056 --> 41:35.236 align:middle
to an end at that point.

41:35.236 --> 41:43.526 align:middle
We don't have any expertise in
managing pools of credit assets,

41:43.826 --> 41:47.756 align:middle
loans-if you will-or bonds,
and we, you know, we don't want

41:47.756 --> 41:50.236 align:middle
to be made-we don't really want
to be part of the decisions

41:50.236 --> 41:52.626 align:middle
that have to be made to
manage such a portfolio,

41:52.626 --> 41:57.546 align:middle
so we'd be looking to have that
done either someplace else or,

41:58.056 --> 42:00.196 align:middle
you know, by-by a third party

42:00.196 --> 42:01.586 align:middle
or at the Treasury
Department or something.

42:01.586 --> 42:03.476 align:middle
And we're, you know, we're
working on ideas for that.

42:03.836 --> 42:07.516 align:middle
But our real focus now is on-is
getting these facilities going

42:07.516 --> 42:10.296 align:middle
and-and getting them to do the
job that they-they need to do.

42:10.296 --> 42:13.886 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:15.796 --> 42:17.216 align:middle
Thank you.

42:17.406 --> 42:19.236 align:middle
Jonelle with Reuters.

42:19.616 --> 42:20.686 align:middle
JONELLE MARTE.

42:21.326 --> 42:22.086 align:middle
Thank you, Chair.

42:22.236 --> 42:23.946 align:middle
So I have two quick questions.

42:23.946 --> 42:26.826 align:middle
One is if we have hit
bottom for the economy,

42:26.826 --> 42:30.516 align:middle
and the second question
is regarding the-the way

42:30.516 --> 42:34.096 align:middle
that the pandemic has
exacerbated racial inequality.

42:34.716 --> 42:38.636 align:middle
I wanted to ask, to what extent
do you think you can factor

42:38.636 --> 42:41.046 align:middle
in both disparities
when you're thinking

42:41.046 --> 42:42.236 align:middle
about maximum employment?

42:42.236 --> 42:42.566 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:42.566 --> 42:48.676 align:middle
I would say that many
forecasters had been expecting a

42:48.676 --> 42:51.576 align:middle
bottom for the economy in-around
the middle of the year,

42:51.746 --> 42:53.536 align:middle
with-with a huge
range of uncertainty.

42:54.216 --> 42:58.546 align:middle
I think the labor market-the
evidence of one jobs report is

42:58.546 --> 43:01.656 align:middle
that the labor market may
have hit bottom in May.

43:01.656 --> 43:03.506 align:middle
We don't know that;
we're going to see.

43:04.096 --> 43:09.666 align:middle
And, you know, many forecasters
widely expect a-a recovery

43:09.666 --> 43:10.896 align:middle
over the second half
of the year.

43:10.896 --> 43:14.156 align:middle
So it-it is possible, but
the thing is, we're not going

43:14.156 --> 43:15.866 align:middle
to overreact to a
single data point.

43:16.546 --> 43:19.396 align:middle
We're going to be very careful

43:19.486 --> 43:23.576 align:middle
about reaching any conclusions
about-about good data

43:23.576 --> 43:24.306 align:middle
or bad data.

43:24.356 --> 43:26.116 align:middle
So, I think, you know,
we're going to be here

43:26.116 --> 43:27.896 align:middle
with our tools supporting
this economy

43:28.366 --> 43:29.436 align:middle
for as long as it's needed.

43:29.436 --> 43:32.876 align:middle
So-but I think there's a
possibility that-that the bottom

43:32.876 --> 43:35.926 align:middle
has come in the labor market,
but we don't know that yet.

43:35.926 --> 43:38.786 align:middle
We'll-we'll know more
as we go forward.

43:39.286 --> 43:43.106 align:middle
So, in terms of the-you know,
the effect of the pandemic

43:43.106 --> 43:48.356 align:middle
on inequality, what you see-
the pandemic, of course,

43:48.676 --> 43:52.236 align:middle
hits everybody, but
in an economic sense,

43:52.376 --> 43:56.466 align:middle
it hits those industries
that are-that involve groups

43:56.466 --> 44:01.426 align:middle
of people in tight
groups, either in places

44:01.426 --> 44:05.656 align:middle
where they're-it's the travel,
leisure, restaurant, bars,

44:06.176 --> 44:10.186 align:middle
those kinds of service-economy
jobs.

44:10.776 --> 44:16.286 align:middle
And so a lot of the lost jobs
were from people who work

44:16.286 --> 44:19.496 align:middle
in the service economy, dealing
with the public, for example,

44:19.836 --> 44:23.766 align:middle
and relatively-compared to other
jobs, relatively low wages.

44:24.386 --> 44:27.716 align:middle
So, and if-if you just
look at what that is,

44:28.286 --> 44:30.996 align:middle
unemployment has gone
up more for Hispanics,

44:30.996 --> 44:33.246 align:middle
more for African Americans.

44:33.366 --> 44:38.596 align:middle
And women have borne-borne an
extraordinary-a notable share

44:38.596 --> 44:40.866 align:middle
of the burden beyond their
percentage in the workforce.

44:41.386 --> 44:45.916 align:middle
So that's really-really,
really unfortunate,

44:46.006 --> 44:48.956 align:middle
because if you just go back
two months, where we were was,

44:49.546 --> 44:51.946 align:middle
we had, effectively, the
first tight labor market

44:51.946 --> 44:53.106 align:middle
in a quarter-century.

44:53.106 --> 44:56.886 align:middle
And for the last couple of
years before the pandemic hit,

44:56.886 --> 45:00.886 align:middle
you were seeing wages go up the
most for people at the lower end

45:01.156 --> 45:03.016 align:middle
of the wage spectrum,
and that was great.

45:03.016 --> 45:06.276 align:middle
And, you know, we were meeting
with people from low- and-low-

45:06.276 --> 45:08.546 align:middle
and moderate-income
communities all over the country

45:08.546 --> 45:10.506 align:middle
and hearing, "Don't change this.

45:10.616 --> 45:12.026 align:middle
Do whatever you-do
whatever you can.

45:12.026 --> 45:12.736 align:middle
Keep this going.

45:12.736 --> 45:14.366 align:middle
This is the best labor
market we've seen."

45:14.786 --> 45:17.486 align:middle
And we had every expect-every
expectation, every reason

45:17.486 --> 45:20.536 align:middle
to expect that this would
continue, and then this comes.

45:20.536 --> 45:23.406 align:middle
So it's-it's heartbreaking,
and, you know,

45:23.406 --> 45:25.926 align:middle
we want to get it back, so-we
really want to get it back.

45:25.926 --> 45:30.676 align:middle
And so I think we learned a
number of things over the course

45:30.676 --> 45:31.446 align:middle
of the last few years.

45:31.446 --> 45:37.046 align:middle
One of them is that, you
know, I-you-you can have 3,

45:37.046 --> 45:39.816 align:middle
3½ percent unemployment for a
couple of years, and, really,

45:40.496 --> 45:42.336 align:middle
you see modest moves in wages

45:42.336 --> 45:46.986 align:middle
and relatively almost
invisible moves in inflation.

45:47.506 --> 45:50.766 align:middle
So that was not anybody's
understanding of the structure

45:50.766 --> 45:52.846 align:middle
of the economy, I think,
or most peoples', anyway.

45:52.846 --> 45:57.556 align:middle
So we can use our tools to-to
support the labor market

45:57.556 --> 45:58.606 align:middle
and support the economy.

45:59.446 --> 46:02.836 align:middle
We can-we can use them
until we do fully recover,

46:02.836 --> 46:04.536 align:middle
and that-that's what
we're planning to do.

46:04.536 --> 46:07.636 align:middle
You know, we don't
target different groups.

46:07.636 --> 46:09.996 align:middle
What-what we are-we are
cognizant of the fact, though,

46:09.996 --> 46:14.466 align:middle
that late in the last cycle,
late in the last expansion,

46:14.946 --> 46:17.986 align:middle
the benefits really do go more
to people at the lower end

46:17.986 --> 46:20.886 align:middle
of the wage spectrum for-for
the first time in many years

46:21.656 --> 46:24.496 align:middle
when labor markets are tight,
when unemployment is low.

46:24.696 --> 46:26.696 align:middle
And so we would really
like to get back

46:27.296 --> 46:30.946 align:middle
to that-get back to that place.

46:33.696 --> 46:34.016 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:35.836 --> 46:38.896 align:middle
Victoria from Politico.

46:39.266 --> 46:41.006 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

46:41.006 --> 46:42.756 align:middle
Hi, I'm Victoria
Guida with Politico.

46:42.756 --> 46:44.806 align:middle
I just wanted to follow
up on a couple of things

46:44.806 --> 46:46.576 align:middle
that are-have already
been asked.

46:46.576 --> 46:50.106 align:middle
First of all, on fiscal
policy, you all obviously put

46:50.106 --> 46:52.546 align:middle
out your Summary of
Economic Projections today,

46:52.986 --> 46:57.266 align:middle
and I'm curious to what extent
future fiscal policy is factored

46:57.266 --> 47:01.926 align:middle
in or not factored in to those
projections, and-and how,

47:01.926 --> 47:04.886 align:middle
more or less, fiscal policy
might affect those projections.

47:05.196 --> 47:08.756 align:middle
And then my other question
is on-on Main Street.

47:09.076 --> 47:12.266 align:middle
You've mentioned multiple
times that, you know, the Fed

47:12.266 --> 47:15.256 align:middle
and Treasury are willing to
expand the program further.

47:15.256 --> 47:17.976 align:middle
So my question is,
what's the threshold

47:17.976 --> 47:19.636 align:middle
for those types of changes?

47:19.636 --> 47:24.006 align:middle
Is it just making sure that
borrowers that want to borrow

47:24.006 --> 47:26.226 align:middle
through the program
are-are-are able to do that?

47:26.326 --> 47:26.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:26.776 --> 47:29.406 align:middle
In terms of the way-the
way we do forecasts is,

47:29.406 --> 47:33.556 align:middle
we-we don't-the-we don't tend

47:34.356 --> 47:37.286 align:middle
to incorporate things we're
highly uncertain about.

47:37.286 --> 47:40.106 align:middle
So I think the-the forecast
would not have included

47:40.746 --> 47:42.636 align:middle
substantial additional-you know,

47:42.636 --> 47:45.006 align:middle
big additional fiscal
support for the economy.

47:45.006 --> 47:47.776 align:middle
Maybe-maybe a modest
amount, something that looks

47:47.776 --> 47:50.396 align:middle
like a-a low-end guess
on what might come

47:50.396 --> 47:51.516 align:middle
out of the current negotiations.

47:51.516 --> 47:53.726 align:middle
That's basically what
would be in the baseline.

47:53.726 --> 47:56.066 align:middle
So, of course, if there
were more fiscal support,

47:56.066 --> 47:59.236 align:middle
you'd see-you'd see
better results sooner,

47:59.386 --> 48:00.616 align:middle
but that's a question
for Congress.

48:00.616 --> 48:02.036 align:middle
You know, we're-we're
spending a lot,

48:02.036 --> 48:04.926 align:middle
and that's really what
they get to decide.

48:05.626 --> 48:08.086 align:middle
In terms of Main Street
and our willingness

48:08.086 --> 48:10.446 align:middle
to expand it further, I think
we're-one thing we're looking

48:10.446 --> 48:14.576 align:middle
at-very strongly looking
at is-is nonprofits,

48:15.096 --> 48:19.676 align:middle
and is there a way to-to
incorporate them into-into

48:19.836 --> 48:22.006 align:middle
that facility or a
similar facility.

48:22.006 --> 48:23.386 align:middle
So that's another dimension.

48:23.946 --> 48:26.956 align:middle
I-you know, if we
had a great idea

48:26.956 --> 48:28.596 align:middle
for changing Main Street,
we would've done it.

48:28.596 --> 48:30.296 align:middle
And we-and we have
done some things

48:30.296 --> 48:32.626 align:middle
that I think are really
very positive lately here.

48:33.176 --> 48:36.846 align:middle
But if we-as we learn more, it
could be-it could be in terms

48:36.846 --> 48:39.196 align:middle
of size, it could be in terms
of lots of different things.

48:39.786 --> 48:42.746 align:middle
But I-I think we have a
good product to go to market

48:42.746 --> 48:46.606 align:middle
with now, and I think it'll
get-it'll get out there soon.

48:46.606 --> 48:47.826 align:middle
And, you know, we'll see,

48:47.946 --> 48:52.856 align:middle
and-and we'll be willing
to continue to adapt.

48:53.306 --> 48:53.916 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

48:56.236 --> 48:56.746 align:middle
Thank you.

48:56.826 --> 48:58.756 align:middle
Don Lee. DON LEE.

48:58.756 --> 49:04.986 align:middle
Chair Powell, as you know,
the vast majority of people

49:05.326 --> 49:08.386 align:middle
who have been laid
off are expecting

49:08.386 --> 49:10.636 align:middle
to be recalled by
their employers.

49:11.276 --> 49:15.626 align:middle
At this point, what is your
expectation of how much

49:15.626 --> 49:18.556 align:middle
of these job losses
will be permanent?

49:18.896 --> 49:19.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:19.516 --> 49:21.476 align:middle
I don't have a reliable
estimate of that.

49:21.766 --> 49:23.316 align:middle
Clearly, not everyone
will go back.

49:23.816 --> 49:29.356 align:middle
But-and I-I would say, many will
go back, but what's-what's going

49:29.356 --> 49:33.706 align:middle
to be the remainder when-you
know, when we reach sort

49:33.706 --> 49:34.786 align:middle
of what is the new normal?

49:34.786 --> 49:37.386 align:middle
It's-it's so uncertain, but
it-it could be a good number

49:37.386 --> 49:40.946 align:middle
of millions of people, I think,
in many-in many estimates,

49:40.946 --> 49:43.086 align:middle
but these-I'd say you're
so early in the process.

49:43.086 --> 49:45.996 align:middle
People are-for example, people
are going back and looking

49:45.996 --> 49:50.706 align:middle
at other significant
changes in the economy,

49:50.706 --> 49:53.836 align:middle
and they've seen how many people
go back when, for example,

49:53.836 --> 49:54.986 align:middle
there's a technological change,

49:54.986 --> 49:56.236 align:middle
and things like that-you
may have seen some

49:56.236 --> 49:58.456 align:middle
of this research-and you
come up with an estimate.

49:58.996 --> 50:01.056 align:middle
I-you know, it's just going
to be very hard to say.

50:01.276 --> 50:04.316 align:middle
I-I, but I-my-my assumption is

50:04.316 --> 50:07.366 align:middle
that there will be a
significant chunk-chunk well

50:07.366 --> 50:08.196 align:middle
into the millions.

50:08.846 --> 50:10.896 align:middle
I-I don't want to give you
a number, because it's going

50:10.896 --> 50:13.866 align:middle
to be a guess, but well,
well into the millions

50:14.096 --> 50:17.726 align:middle
of people who-who don't get
to go back to their old job.

50:17.806 --> 50:21.466 align:middle
And, in fact, there isn't
a-there may not be a job

50:21.466 --> 50:23.376 align:middle
in that industry for
them for some time.

50:23.376 --> 50:24.506 align:middle
There will eventually be,

50:24.506 --> 50:27.486 align:middle
but it could be some
years before we get back

50:27.486 --> 50:29.976 align:middle
to those people finding jobs.

50:29.976 --> 50:33.206 align:middle
I mean, when-when people lose
a job, if they can find a job

50:33.206 --> 50:35.596 align:middle
in their own industry, that's
usually the fastest way,

50:35.966 --> 50:38.386 align:middle
is-is they know other
people in that industry,

50:38.386 --> 50:39.326 align:middle
different kinds of jobs.

50:39.326 --> 50:40.246 align:middle
That's usually the fastest.

50:40.246 --> 50:43.466 align:middle
If you have to go to a different
industry and start over again,

50:43.826 --> 50:45.386 align:middle
it's much harder, and
that's where you start

50:45.386 --> 50:48.416 align:middle
to lose people's-who are-just
fall out of the labor force.

50:48.416 --> 50:51.136 align:middle
And-and, you know, it's
very tough on their lives.

50:51.646 --> 50:53.556 align:middle
We all know people
who-to whom that happened

50:53.556 --> 50:56.936 align:middle
in the Global Financial
Crisis, and, hence,

50:56.936 --> 50:59.836 align:middle
our desire to-to do what we can

50:59.836 --> 51:03.976 align:middle
to support this and-and-and
support this recovery with-with

51:03.976 --> 51:04.686 align:middle
the tools that we have.

51:05.286 --> 51:06.186 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

51:08.096 --> 51:08.396 align:middle
Thank you.

51:08.396 --> 51:11.256 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer.

51:11.256 --> 51:12.476 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

51:13.986 --> 51:17.606 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer,
Marketplace.

51:18.836 --> 51:21.056 align:middle
Chair Powell, I'm wondering-I
want to go back to the issue

51:21.056 --> 51:22.996 align:middle
of inequality, and
I'm wondering,

51:23.576 --> 51:25.046 align:middle
what more could the Fed do

51:25.046 --> 51:28.166 align:middle
to reduce economic
inequality in this country?

51:28.166 --> 51:30.956 align:middle
I mean, I know that you said
you don't target certain groups,

51:30.996 --> 51:34.106 align:middle
but is there a way that
you could use, for example,

51:34.106 --> 51:37.276 align:middle
the black unemployment rate
as-as some kind of a benchmark

51:37.326 --> 51:40.266 align:middle
to something you'd want
to meet or something

51:40.266 --> 51:41.076 align:middle
that you'd keep track of?

51:41.076 --> 51:41.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

51:41.336 --> 51:44.116 align:middle
We do. We do track all
unemployment by all kinds,

51:44.116 --> 51:46.726 align:middle
as you know-all kinds of
different demographics,

51:46.726 --> 51:48.806 align:middle
including particularly the
African American unemployment

51:48.806 --> 51:52.556 align:middle
rate, which reached
an all-time low

51:53.006 --> 51:55.746 align:middle
since the data started being
kept in the modern era.

51:56.446 --> 51:58.496 align:middle
Of course, it's still close

51:58.496 --> 52:00.336 align:middle
to twice the white
unemployment-it is twice the

52:00.336 --> 52:03.766 align:middle
unemployment rate, or it
was back in the-back then.

52:04.056 --> 52:05.646 align:middle
It's certainly much higher now.

52:06.306 --> 52:08.846 align:middle
You know, the-the
best thing we can do

52:08.846 --> 52:14.226 align:middle
with our monetary policy tools
is to-is to look at, you know,

52:15.386 --> 52:17.876 align:middle
the evidence of what we saw-that
we saw with our own eyes

52:17.876 --> 52:20.776 align:middle
so recently, which is that
the economy can have very low

52:20.776 --> 52:22.636 align:middle
unemployment-very
low unemployment.

52:23.126 --> 52:24.376 align:middle
It could've been
lower than we were.

52:24.376 --> 52:25.806 align:middle
It could be lower
than 3½ percent

52:25.806 --> 52:28.096 align:middle
without seeing financial
imbalances,

52:28.096 --> 52:31.626 align:middle
without seeing inflation
going-getting out of control.

52:31.996 --> 52:33.696 align:middle
We, frankly, didn't
even get inflation back

52:33.696 --> 52:36.656 align:middle
up to target-without
seeing wages getting

52:36.866 --> 52:39.026 align:middle
out of touch with-with
where they should be,

52:39.416 --> 52:41.516 align:middle
so that's the-that's the
biggest thing we can do.

52:41.756 --> 52:44.696 align:middle
You know, in-in-inequality
is something that's been

52:45.726 --> 52:49.116 align:middle
with us increasingly for
more than four decades,

52:49.116 --> 52:52.096 align:middle
and it's-it's not really
related to monetary policy.

52:52.096 --> 52:55.876 align:middle
It's, or-or it's more
related to-there are a lot

52:55.876 --> 52:57.426 align:middle
of theories on-on
what causes it,

52:57.426 --> 53:00.526 align:middle
but it's been something
that's more or less been going

53:01.136 --> 53:03.586 align:middle
up consistently for
more than four decades.

53:03.586 --> 53:05.386 align:middle
And there are a lot
of different theories,

53:05.386 --> 53:09.826 align:middle
one of which is-just is that
it-globalization-globalization

53:09.826 --> 53:13.196 align:middle
and technology call for rising
levels of skills and aptitudes

53:13.396 --> 53:17.386 align:middle
and education, and that U.S.
educational attainment kind

53:17.716 --> 53:19.396 align:middle
of flattened out,
certainly relative

53:19.396 --> 53:22.056 align:middle
to our peers-flattened
out over that period.

53:22.686 --> 53:26.206 align:middle
And so a lot-so that
means if you're-if you're

53:26.206 --> 53:28.446 align:middle
on the right side
of those trends,

53:29.116 --> 53:31.486 align:middle
then those things
are good for you.

53:31.486 --> 53:33.586 align:middle
If you're not, then your
wages are going to stagnate.

53:33.586 --> 53:35.916 align:middle
And wages for the
bottom, you know,

53:35.916 --> 53:37.766 align:middle
10 percent really haven't
gone up in real terms

53:37.766 --> 53:40.746 align:middle
in a very long time, and-over
a long period of time,

53:40.746 --> 53:43.346 align:middle
whereas wages for the people
at the top, compensation,

53:43.716 --> 53:47.166 align:middle
any way you cut it-before taxes,
after taxes, after transfers,

53:47.766 --> 53:50.976 align:middle
all of those, any way you cut
it-wages have-compensation has

53:50.976 --> 53:52.456 align:middle
gone up a whole lot
for people at the top,

53:52.456 --> 53:54.366 align:middle
and it really hasn't gone
up for people at the bottom.

53:54.906 --> 53:57.326 align:middle
If you look more in the middle,
then it's-then-then it has gone

53:57.326 --> 53:59.416 align:middle
up for most other
groups, but at the bottom,

53:59.416 --> 54:01.596 align:middle
not so much in real terms,
in inflation-adjusted terms.

54:01.596 --> 54:05.856 align:middle
So, you know, we-we call it
out as a-as an important factor

54:05.856 --> 54:08.506 align:middle
in the economy, and
we will use our tools

54:08.506 --> 54:11.916 align:middle
to support maximum
employment and take that,

54:12.026 --> 54:13.856 align:middle
you know, definition to heart.

54:14.506 --> 54:16.576 align:middle
But, obviously, that's
something that's going

54:16.576 --> 54:20.026 align:middle
to require an all-of-society,
all-of-government response.

54:20.076 --> 54:20.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

54:26.036 --> 54:26.476 align:middle
Thank you.

54:26.476 --> 54:27.856 align:middle
For the last question, we'll go

54:27.856 --> 54:29.536 align:middle
to Michael McKee
with Bloomberg TV.

54:29.536 --> 54:29.856 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

54:31.186 --> 54:34.326 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Michael McKee,
Bloomberg Television and Radio.

54:34.326 --> 54:37.596 align:middle
I came across a statistic
the other day that amazed me.

54:37.596 --> 54:42.496 align:middle
Since your March 23 emergency
announcement, every single stock

54:42.496 --> 54:46.066 align:middle
in the S&amp;P 500 has
delivered a positive return.

54:46.386 --> 54:49.556 align:middle
And I'm wondering, given the
levels of the market right now,

54:49.896 --> 54:53.526 align:middle
whether you or your colleagues
feel there is a possible bubble

54:53.656 --> 54:58.406 align:middle
blowing that could pop and set
back the recovery significantly,

54:58.776 --> 55:01.086 align:middle
or that we might see
capital misallocation

55:01.086 --> 55:03.576 align:middle
that will leave us worse
off when this is over.

55:03.576 --> 55:07.746 align:middle
Second, inequality is
not just about wages,

55:07.746 --> 55:09.216 align:middle
it's also about wealth.

55:09.216 --> 55:10.966 align:middle
And a number of studies
have suggested

55:10.966 --> 55:14.436 align:middle
that by keeping rates low for so
long and targeting the markets

55:14.436 --> 55:16.176 align:middle
after the great financial
crisis,

55:16.366 --> 55:18.526 align:middle
that the Fed did contribute

55:18.636 --> 55:20.946 align:middle
to wealth inequality
in this country.

55:20.946 --> 55:23.546 align:middle
And I'm wondering if you
think there is some tweak

55:23.786 --> 55:27.566 align:middle
or some message you could
give that would affect that.

55:28.466 --> 55:28.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

55:28.896 --> 55:32.246 align:middle
What we've targeted is
broader financial conditions.

55:32.246 --> 55:35.736 align:middle
If you go back to the end
of February and early March,

55:35.806 --> 55:42.596 align:middle
you had-basically, the world
markets realized at just

55:42.596 --> 55:48.186 align:middle
about the same time-I remember
that Monday-that there was going

55:48.186 --> 55:50.596 align:middle
to be a global pandemic,
and that this possibility

55:50.596 --> 55:52.876 align:middle
that it would be contained
in one province in China,

55:53.076 --> 55:55.446 align:middle
for all practical purposes,
was not going to happen.

55:55.946 --> 55:59.506 align:middle
It all-it was-you know,
it was Iran, Italy, Korea,

56:00.086 --> 56:01.746 align:middle
and then it became
clear in markets.

56:01.936 --> 56:03.746 align:middle
From that point forward,
investors everywhere

56:03.746 --> 56:07.446 align:middle
in the world, for a period of
weeks, wanted to sell everything

56:07.446 --> 56:11.696 align:middle
that wasn't cash or a-a
short-term Treasury instrument.

56:11.936 --> 56:14.166 align:middle
They didn't want it to
have any risk at all.

56:14.566 --> 56:16.396 align:middle
And so what happened is,
markets stopped working.

56:16.546 --> 56:17.396 align:middle
They stopped working,

56:18.416 --> 56:21.216 align:middle
and companies couldn't-couldn't
borrow.

56:21.216 --> 56:23.066 align:middle
They couldn't roll
over their debt.

56:23.066 --> 56:24.006 align:middle
People couldn't borrow.

56:24.006 --> 56:26.316 align:middle
So that's-that's the
kind of situation

56:26.316 --> 56:31.036 align:middle
that can be very-financial
turbulence

56:31.036 --> 56:34.626 align:middle
and malfunction-a financial
system that's not working can

56:34.706 --> 56:37.636 align:middle
greatly amplify the
negative effects

56:37.706 --> 56:40.566 align:middle
of what was clearly going to
be a major economic shock.

56:41.146 --> 56:43.956 align:middle
So what our tools were-were
put to work to do was

56:43.956 --> 56:45.616 align:middle
to restore the markets
to function.

56:46.326 --> 56:48.736 align:middle
And I think, you know, some
of that has really happened,

56:48.736 --> 56:51.226 align:middle
as I-as I mentioned
in my opening remarks,

56:51.606 --> 56:52.516 align:middle
and that's a good thing.

56:52.516 --> 56:54.306 align:middle
So we-we're not looking

56:54.306 --> 56:56.786 align:middle
to achieve a particular
level of any asset price.

56:56.786 --> 57:00.206 align:middle
What we want is investors
to be pricing in risk

57:00.546 --> 57:01.976 align:middle
like markets are supposed to do.

57:02.386 --> 57:03.346 align:middle
Borrowers are borrowing.

57:03.346 --> 57:04.046 align:middle
Lenders are lending.

57:04.046 --> 57:05.536 align:middle
We want the markets
to be working.

57:06.016 --> 57:08.526 align:middle
And, again, we're not looking
to-to a particular level.

57:09.556 --> 57:13.986 align:middle
I think our-our principal focus,
though, is on-on the state

57:13.986 --> 57:17.296 align:middle
of the economy and on the
labor market and on inflation.

57:17.296 --> 57:19.346 align:middle
Now, inflation, of
course, is-is low,

57:19.746 --> 57:21.806 align:middle
and we think it's very
likely to remain low

57:21.806 --> 57:23.186 align:middle
for some time, below our target.

57:23.186 --> 57:27.506 align:middle
So, really, it's about
getting the labor market back

57:27.646 --> 57:28.586 align:middle
and getting it in shape.

57:28.586 --> 57:30.126 align:middle
That's-that's been
our major focus.

57:30.126 --> 57:32.816 align:middle
And I would say, you
know, we-if we were

57:32.816 --> 57:35.896 align:middle
to hold back because-we
would never do this,

57:35.956 --> 57:40.166 align:middle
but-the idea that, just the
concept that we would hold back

57:40.456 --> 57:44.936 align:middle
because we think asset prices
are too high-others may not

57:44.936 --> 57:46.226 align:middle
think so, but we just decided

57:46.226 --> 57:48.926 align:middle
that that's the case-what
would happen to those people?

57:49.236 --> 57:50.196 align:middle
You know, what would
happen to the people

57:50.196 --> 57:52.326 align:middle
that we're actually legally
supposed to be serving?

57:52.326 --> 57:54.176 align:middle
We're supposed to be
pursuing maximum employment

57:54.176 --> 57:56.656 align:middle
and stable prices, and
that's what we're pursuing.

57:57.066 --> 57:58.866 align:middle
We're also pursuing
financial stability,

57:58.916 --> 58:02.166 align:middle
but there you have a
banking system that is

58:02.466 --> 58:06.026 align:middle
so much better capitalized,
so much stronger, better aware

58:06.026 --> 58:09.296 align:middle
of its risks, better at managing
its risks, more highly liquid.

58:09.806 --> 58:11.256 align:middle
You have all of those things.

58:11.256 --> 58:12.136 align:middle
And they've been lending.

58:12.136 --> 58:13.766 align:middle
They've been taking in deposits.

58:14.086 --> 58:16.146 align:middle
They've been a source of
strength in this situation.

58:16.146 --> 58:19.156 align:middle
So I would say that, you
know, we're tightly focused

58:19.156 --> 58:20.696 align:middle
on our real economy goals.

58:21.376 --> 58:24.826 align:middle
And-and, again, not-we're
not-we're not focused

58:24.826 --> 58:28.296 align:middle
on moving asset prices in a
particular direction at all.

58:28.326 --> 58:30.886 align:middle
It's just we want markets
to be working, and I think,

58:31.206 --> 58:33.386 align:middle
partly as a result of what we've
done, they-they are working.

58:33.386 --> 58:35.456 align:middle
And, you know, we
hope that continues.

58:36.596 --> 58:37.646 align:middle
Thank you very much.

